# Get Started

## Overview

<div class="at-a-glance" markdown>

**In short:** Umucyo is a digital platform that helps tea cooperatives manage tea
collection, keep accurate records of farmers and their deliveries, and pay farmers
every month.

**Who it is for:** one cooperative at a time — an administrator at a desk, agents at
collection points, and farmers with basic phones.

**What it replaces:** paper delivery books, a spreadsheet of prices, and a month-end
reconciliation done by hand.

</div>

### The problem

Tea cooperatives collect green tea leaves from many farmers at several collection
points. Farmers are paid by the weight they deliver, so every weight, price and
payment has to be accurate and traceable. Paper records and informal messages make
that hard: deliveries get lost, prices are unclear, and farmers have little
visibility into what was recorded for them.

#### What that looks like in practice

A delivery book at a collection point records a name, a weight and a date. It does
not record which scale was used, whether that scale had been calibrated, which agent
was on duty, or what the price per kilogram was on that day. Weeks later, when a
farmer disputes a payment, none of those questions can be answered from the book.
The honest answer is usually "somewhere between these two numbers".

Three specific failures follow from that:

| Failure | How it shows up | Cost |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Lost deliveries** | A page is torn, a book is left at the wrong point, a name is written in a way nobody can match later | A farmer is not paid for work already done |
| **Unclear prices** | The price changes mid-month but nobody wrote down the exact date it changed | Two farmers with identical deliveries are paid differently, and neither can be explained |
| **No visibility** | A farmer has no way to check their own record without travelling to the point and asking | Disputes become conversations about trust instead of about records |

#### Why a spreadsheet does not fix it

A spreadsheet is often the first attempt, and it fails for structural reasons rather
than lazy ones. Anyone with the file can edit any row, nothing records who changed
what, two people cannot safely enter deliveries at the same time, and there is no
link between a delivery and the payment that settled it. The information is there,
but it cannot be trusted enough to pay from.

#### Why a phone menu, not an app, for farmers

Most farmers do not carry a smartphone. Requiring one would exclude the people the
system exists for. That single constraint explains several decisions elsewhere in
this documentation: why farmers interact through USSD and SMS, why delivery
confirmations are short enough to read on a small screen, and why the agent app is
built to work on a cheap Android phone with a poor connection.

### What Umucyo does

- Registers the people involved in a controlled way: administrators, agents and farmers.
- Records every delivery against a verified scale, a collection point and the agent who weighed it.
- Calculates the amount owed from the weight and the official price per kilogram.
- Pays farmers monthly through M-Pesa.
- Keeps farmers informed by SMS, and gives them access to their information by USSD from any basic phone.
- Lets administrators assign and track work for their agents.

Each of those is a feature with its own page. Stated as a chain, they describe one
delivery from field to payment:

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    A[Farmer brings<br/>leaf to point] --> B[Agent registers<br/>farmer once]
    B --> C[Scale records<br/>gross weight]
    C --> D[Backend stores<br/>the delivery]
    D --> E[SMS confirms<br/>weight to farmer]
    D --> F[Price in force<br/>on that date applied]
    F --> G[Month-end<br/>payment batch]
    G --> H[Money to the<br/>farmer's phone]
    D --> I[Farmer checks<br/>record by USSD]
```

#### The rules the system holds to

Four rules run through every feature. They are stated here because they explain
behaviour that otherwise looks arbitrary.

| Rule | What it means | Where you will see it |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **The device is the record** | A weight comes from a registered scale, not from a typed number | Deliveries cannot be edited after they are saved |
| **The price is a period** | A delivery is valued at the price in force on its own date, not at today's price | Prices are stored as date ranges that may not overlap |
| **Retrying is safe** | Sending the same request twice cannot create two records | A dropped connection mid-save is never a double delivery |
| **Every query is scoped** | Each record belongs to exactly one cooperative and is never visible across that line | An agent sees only their own collection point |

### Who uses it

| User | Uses | Main jobs |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Cooperative administrator | Admin dashboard (web) | Manage collection points, agents, devices, prices and tasks; pay farmers |
| Collection agent | Agent app (installable web app) | Register farmers, record deliveries, complete tasks |
| Farmer | Basic phone (SMS and USSD) | Receive delivery confirmations, check reports |

#### The same system, seen from three sides

**The administrator** works at a desk and cares about the month: are all deliveries
in, is the price right, did every farmer get paid, and who is still owed money. The
dashboard is built for reading and deciding, so it favours tables, totals and a
preview before anything irreversible happens.

**The agent** works standing up, often outdoors, with one hand free. The app is built
for doing: large buttons, few fields, a save that survives a weak signal, and a clear
statement of what the farmer will receive by SMS.

**The farmer** holds a basic phone and wants three answers: what did I deliver, what
is it worth, and when do I get paid. Anything longer than a few lines of SMS will not
be read on a small screen.

### What Umucyo is not

Saying what a system does not do prevents a great deal of confusion later.

- **Not an accounting package.** It records what is owed and what was paid. It does
  not produce a full set of financial statements.
- **Not a farm-management tool.** It does not record planting, weeding, fertiliser or
  yield per plot. It starts at the collection point.
- **Not a public service.** There is no farmer sign-up and no public price page.
  Access is by invitation and by phone number.
- **Not multi-cooperative by default.** One deployment serves one cooperative. The
  records are scoped so that the boundary is real, not so that several co-ops can
  share an account.
- **Not an offline-first system.** The agent app tolerates a brief drop, but it needs
  a connection to confirm a delivery. Background syncing is not built.

### Where the system is heading

The roadmap is not a promise, but it sets the direction of the work:

| Area | Next step | Why it is next |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Offline | Queue deliveries while disconnected, send when signal returns | Agents work in places where signal is unreliable |
| Payments | Reconciliation report from the provider, not only from our own records | The failure that matters is paying twice or not at all |
| Reporting | Monthly yield per farmer and per point | The cooperative currently builds this by hand |
| Devices | Calibration log per scale | A disputed weight is a trust problem |

### Words you will meet

A few terms recur throughout this documentation and are worth fixing early:

- **Collection point** — where tea is brought and weighed.
- **Produce record / delivery** — one weighing of one farmer's leaf; the same thing.
- **Price period** — the date range a price applies to.
- **Batch** — the group of payments prepared together for one month.
- **PWA** — the installable web app the agent uses.

The [glossary](#glossary) defines the rest.

### Where to go next

- [Getting started](#getting-started) to run the system on your computer.
- [Architecture](#architecture) to see how the parts fit together.
- [Features](features/index.md) for how each part of the system works.
- [Glossary](#glossary) when a word in another page is unfamiliar.

If you are reading this as an assessor or a new team member, the shortest path is
Overview → Getting started → Architecture, then the feature page for whatever you
have been asked about.

## Getting Started

<div class="at-a-glance" markdown>

**In short:** start the backend first on port 8000, then each web app with `npm run dev`. Local work uses mock payments, so nothing is ever sent to M-Pesa.

**You need three terminals, four tools and about twenty minutes.** When you finish this page you will have a working cooperative, an administrator account, and a delivery you created yourself.

</div>

### How the three pieces fit together

Umucyo is three separate programs that talk to one database. Nothing works until the backend is running, because both web apps ask it for everything.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    A["sulwe-dashboard<br/>port 3000"] -->|HTTP + token| B["sulwe-backend<br/>port 8000"]
    C["sulwe-frontend<br/>port 3001"] -->|HTTP + token| B
    B --> D[("PostgreSQL")]
    B --> E["MTN MoMo<br/>(mocked locally)"]
    B --> F["SMS provider<br/>(mocked locally)"]
```

| Piece | Who uses it | Runs on |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Backend | Nothing directly — it is the API | `8000` |
| Dashboard | Cooperative staff and administrators | `3000` |
| Agent app | Agents who register farmers and record deliveries | `3001` when running together |

The two web apps have no database of their own. If the backend is down, both apps load and then show errors. That is normal and it is the first thing to check when something looks broken.

### Get the code

Umucyo is split into three repositories:

| Repository | What it holds |
| --- | --- |
| [sulwe-backend](https://github.com/akirachix/sulwe-backend) | FastAPI backend |
| [sulwe-dashboard](https://github.com/akirachix/sulwe-dashboard) | Admin dashboard |
| [sulwe-frontend](https://github.com/akirachix/sulwe-frontend) | Agent app |

```bash
git clone https://github.com/akirachix/sulwe-backend.git
git clone https://github.com/akirachix/sulwe-dashboard.git
git clone https://github.com/akirachix/sulwe-frontend.git
```

Keep all three folders next to each other so you can move between them easily:

```bash
mkdir umucyo && cd umucyo
# run the three git clone commands above inside this folder
ls
# sulwe-backend  sulwe-dashboard  sulwe-frontend
```

### What you need

- Python 3.11 and [uv](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/)
- PostgreSQL
- Node.js and npm
- Git

#### Install the tools

Check what you already have before installing anything:

```bash
python3 --version     # want 3.11.x
uv --version
psql --version
node --version        # 18 or newer
npm --version
```

Anything missing:

**macOS**

```bash
brew install python@3.11 uv postgresql@16 node
brew services start postgresql@16
```

**Ubuntu / WSL**

```bash
sudo apt update
sudo apt install python3.11 python3.11-venv postgresql nodejs npm
curl -LsSf https://astral.sh/uv/install.sh | sh
sudo service postgresql start
```

**Windows**

Install Python 3.11 and Node from their websites, PostgreSQL from the
installer, then uv from PowerShell:

```powershell
powershell -c "irm https://astral.sh/uv/install.ps1 | iex"
```

On Windows, run the rest of this page in **WSL** or Git Bash. Every command below is a shell command.

#### Create the database

The backend expects a PostgreSQL database to exist before it starts. It does not create one for you.

```bash
# create a local role and database
createuser -s umucyo --pwprompt
createdb -O umucyo umucyo
```

Confirm you can connect:

```bash
psql -U umucyo -d umucyo -c "SELECT current_database();"
```

### 1. Backend (`sulwe-backend`)

1. Install the packages.
    ```bash
    uv sync
    ```
2. Create a `.env` file with the settings listed in [Configuration](#configuration). Keep `DARAJA_SUBMISSION_MODE=mock`.
3. Create or update the database.
    ```bash
    uv run alembic heads          # must print exactly one head
    uv run alembic upgrade head
    ```
4. Start the server.
    ```bash
    uv run fastapi dev
    ```
5. Open the API documentation at `http://127.0.0.1:8000/docs`.

#### Before step 2: the settings that must be right

A fresh `.env` copied from `.env.example` fails in one of two ways — a wrong database address, or a blank secret. The minimum to get running:

| Setting | Local value | Why |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `DATABASE_URL` | `postgresql://umucyo:umucyo@localhost:5432/umucyo` | Must point at the database you created above |
| `SECRET_KEY` | any long random string | Signs tokens; the backend refuses to start if it is empty |
| `JWT_SECRET_KEY` | any long random string | Signs agent-app tokens |
| `DARAJA_SUBMISSION_MODE` | `mock` | Keeps payments local |
| `ALLOW_SANDBOX_SUBMISSIONS` | `false` | Second guard against real payouts |

Generate a secret rather than inventing one:

```bash
python3 -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(48))"
```

Full list: [Configuration](#configuration).

!!! warning "Back up before changing the database"
    Before `alembic upgrade head` on a database that holds data, take a backup:
    `pg_dump -h localhost -U <db user> -Fc -d <database> -f backup.dump`

#### Step 3: one head, then upgrade

```bash
uv run alembic heads
```

- **Exactly one line** — continue.
- **Two or more lines** — stop. Two developers created migrations from the same starting point and the schema is now ambiguous. Fix it before upgrading, as described in [Database](#database).

Then:

```bash
uv run alembic current    # what is applied now
uv run alembic upgrade head
uv run alembic current    # should now match head
```

#### Step 4: what a good start looks like

```bash
uv run fastapi dev
```

You should see the server bind to `127.0.0.1:8000` and mention reload. Then, in another terminal:

```bash
curl -s http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/v1/health
```

A healthy response returns `200` with a status body. If instead you get `connection refused`, the server is not running. If you get a `500`, read the terminal — almost always `DATABASE_URL`.

!!! tip "The API documentation is the fastest way to check a change"
    Open `http://127.0.0.1:8000/docs`. Every route is listed with its request shape. Sign in with the same token the apps use and you can call anything by hand, without touching a browser.

### 2. Admin dashboard (`sulwe-dashboard`)

1. Install the packages.
    ```bash
    npm install
    ```
2. Create `.env.local` with the backend address (see [Configuration](admin-dashboard)).
3. Start it and open `http://localhost:3000`.
    ```bash
    npm run dev
    ```

The one variable that matters on day one:

```bash
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8000
```

!!! warning "`NEXT_PUBLIC_` values are baked in at build time"
    Changing `.env.local` while `npm run dev` is running is not enough for production builds — rebuild. In development the dev server picks it up on restart. This is the single most common "why is it calling the wrong backend" cause.

### 3. Agent app (`sulwe-frontend`)

Follow the same steps as the dashboard in the `sulwe-frontend` folder. If both apps run at the same time, start the second one on another port: `npm run dev -- -p 3001`.

```bash
cd ../sulwe-frontend
npm install
# .env.local with the same NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL
npm run dev -- -p 3001
```

The agent app is the one to open on a phone or in a narrow browser window. On a real phone, bind to all interfaces so the device can reach your laptop:

```bash
npm run dev -- -p 3001 --hostname 0.0.0.0
```

Then open `http://<your-laptop-ip>:3001` on the phone, on the same network.

### 4. First login

1. Create a cooperative and its first administrator (see [User registration](features/registration.md)).
2. Log in with your email and password.
3. Enter the 6-digit code sent to your email.

#### Where the first administrator comes from

There is no public sign-up — that is deliberate. Someone has to seed the first cooperative and administrator, either:

- through a seed script in the backend, or
- through the Django-style admin or a one-off script run once on your machine.

Once that account exists, every other account is created from inside the dashboard. If you are unsure whether a seed exists in your checkout, look for a script under the backend's scripts or seed folder, or ask the team rather than creating a user by hand in SQL — a hand-written row usually gets the password hash wrong.

!!! tip "Local email codes"
    In local development the one-time code is either printed in the backend terminal or written to the mail log, depending on your `.env`. If no email arrives, look at the backend output first, not your inbox.

### Running everything at once

Three terminals, started in this order:

| Terminal | Folder | Command | Address |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | `sulwe-backend` | `uv run fastapi dev` | `http://127.0.0.1:8000` |
| 2 | `sulwe-dashboard` | `npm run dev` | `http://localhost:3000` |
| 3 | `sulwe-frontend` | `npm run dev -- -p 3001` | `http://localhost:3001` |

Start the backend first. The web apps retry, but you will see avoidable errors if you open them before the API is up.

### Check your setup in five minutes

Work down this list once. If all nine pass, your environment is sound and any later problem is a code problem, not a setup problem.

1. `uv run alembic current` matches `uv run alembic heads`.
2. `http://127.0.0.1:8000/docs` loads.
3. The health endpoint returns `200`.
4. The dashboard loads at `3000` and the sign-in form renders.
5. Signing in with the seeded administrator sends a code.
6. The code arrives (backend terminal or mail log) and logging in succeeds.
7. A collection point can be created and appears in the list.
8. The agent app loads at `3001` and shows its task list.
9. A delivery saved twice with the same idempotency key produces **one** record, not two.

### Every day after this

You do not repeat the setup. The short loop is:

```bash
# terminal 1
cd sulwe-backend && uv run fastapi dev

# terminal 2
cd sulwe-dashboard && npm run dev

# terminal 3
cd sulwe-frontend && npm run dev -- -p 3001
```

After pulling new code, three commands catch most surprises:

```bash
git pull
uv sync                              # backend only: new packages
uv run alembic upgrade head          # backend only: new migrations
npm install                          # each web app: new packages
```

### When it does not work

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `connection refused` on `8000` | Backend not started | Start terminal 1 |
| `500` on every route | Bad `DATABASE_URL` | Check the backend terminal for the psycopg error |
| `password authentication failed` | Wrong role or password in the URL | Re-check the `createdb` / `createuser` step |
| `Target database is not up to date` | Migrations behind | `uv run alembic upgrade head` |
| `Multiple head revisions` | Two migrations branched | Fix the heads before upgrading |
| Login succeeds, every page shows `401` | Token rejected — usually `SECRET_KEY` changed | Sign in again; clear stored tokens |
| Dashboard calls the wrong host | `NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL` wrong, or built earlier | Fix `.env.local`, restart or rebuild |
| App loads but lists are empty | Backend reachable, request failed | Open the browser network tab before assuming no data |
| `Module not found` after a pull | Packages out of date | `uv sync` and `npm install` |
| Port already in use | Another copy still running | Stop it, or use `-p 3002` |

!!! tip "Restart after changing settings"
    The backend reads `.env` and the web apps read `.env.local` only when they start. Restart them after every change.

