## SMS Notifications

<div class="at-a-glance" markdown>

**In short:** when an agent records an accepted delivery, the farmer receives an SMS confirming the weight and the amount, on any phone, with no internet and no app.

**Who receives it:** farmers &nbsp;·&nbsp; **Sent through:** SMS provider (SMS Leopard in the MVP) &nbsp;·&nbsp; **Trigger:** accepted produce record

</div>

### What the SMS contains

- the farmer's name
- the gross weight delivered
- the amount to be paid
- the collection point and the cooperative
- the delivery date

A sample message:

```text
Umucyo: Alice, 14.5 kg accepted at Nyabihu AA on 12/10/2026.
Amount: 4,640. Cooperative: Sulwe.
```

### Why it matters

The SMS is the farmer's **proof**. It is the only part of the system a farmer holds in their hand, so it has to arrive, be readable on a basic phone, and be true.

| Problem without SMS | Effect of the SMS |
| --- | --- |
| Farmer does not know what was recorded | They see weight and amount minutes after delivery |
| Disputes surface at payment time | Disputes surface the same day, when it can still be fixed |
| Trust depends on the agent's word | Trust depends on a message from the cooperative |
| No record for the farmer | The message can be kept as a reference |

### Message rules

!!! info "One message per accepted delivery"
    Rejected deliveries send nothing. Deliveries later edited do not resend automatically; an administrator can resend from the record page.

!!! info "160 characters, no emoji, no links"
    Messages are plain GSM-7 text. Longer messages are truncated at the amount, never mid-number.

!!! info "Language follows the farmer"
    The message uses the farmer's preferred language — English or Kinyarwanda — stored on the farmer record, defaulting to the collection point's language.

### How it works

1. An agent records a delivery with status **accepted**.
2. The record is committed.
3. A background job picks up the record and builds the message text.
4. The job sends to the farmer's phone number through the SMS provider.
5. The provider's response is stored on the record as `sms_status`: `queued`, `sent`, `delivered` or `failed`.
6. A failure is retried three times with backoff, then surfaced on the admin dashboard.

<div class="mermaid" markdown>
flowchart LR
    A[Agent saves accepted record] --> B[Job queued]
    B --> C[Build message in farmer's language]
    C --> D[SMS provider]
    D -->|accepted| E[sms_status = sent]
    D -->|rejected| F[retry x3 with backoff]
    F -->|still failing| G[sms_status = failed, shown to admin]
</div>

!!! note "Sending never blocks the agent"
    The agent's save returns immediately. SMS delivery is asynchronous, so a slow provider cannot stop a queue of farmers at the scale.

### Failure handling

| Situation | What happens |
| --- | --- |
| Provider timeout | Retry after 30 s, then 2 min, then 10 min |
| Provider rejects the number | `failed` with reason `INVALID_NUMBER`, no retry |
| Insufficient credit | `failed` with reason `NO_CREDIT`, alert on dashboard |
| Farmer has no phone | Record saved, `sms_status = skipped` |
| Duplicate trigger | Idempotency key on the record; one message only |

### Settings

| Setting | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `SMS_LEOPARD_API_KEY` | Provider key |
| `SMS_LEOPARD_API_SECRET` | Provider secret |
| `SMS_LEOPARD_SENDER_ID` | Sender name shown to the farmer |
| `SMS_MODE` | `mock` in development, `live` in production |

See [Configuration](#configuration). In `mock` mode messages are written to the log and never sent, so development never spends credit.

### Data stored

SMS itself is not a table of its own; its state lives on the delivery record:

| Field | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `sms_status` | `queued`, `sent`, `delivered`, `failed`, `skipped` |
| `sms_provider_id` | provider's message id, for support queries |
| `sms_sent_at` | when the provider accepted it |
| `sms_error` | reason when failed |

### API endpoints

| Method | Path | Who | What it does |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `POST` | `/produce-records/{id}/resend-sms` | admin | Sends the confirmation again |
| `GET` | `/notifications/sms` | admin | Lists recent messages and their status |
| `POST` | `/webhooks/sms/delivery` | provider | Delivery receipt callback |

### Common errors

| Situation | Visible result |
| --- | --- |
| Provider rejects the number | Record shows `failed / INVALID_NUMBER` |
| No credit | Dashboard banner; records show `failed / NO_CREDIT` |
| Resend on a rejected delivery | **422 SMS_NOT_APPLICABLE** |
| Resend within 60 seconds | **429 SMS_TOO_SOON** |

### Edge cases

- **Farmer shares a phone with a relative.** The message names the farmer, so the reader knows whose delivery it is.
- **Late sync from offline.** The SMS is sent at sync time, not delivery time, and says the delivery date, not the send date.
- **Network outage at the provider.** Messages queue; the provider's own retry handles the rest.
- **Farmer changes number.** The new number gets future messages; old messages are not resent.
- **Very long cooperative or point name.** Truncated so the amount is always intact.

### Testing notes

- **API:** accepted sends, rejected does not, retry path, duplicate suppression.
- **Integration:** mock provider records exactly one call per delivery.
- **E2E:** record delivery → message appears on a test handset.

### Related

- [Produce records](#produce-records)
- [USSD reporting](#ussd-reporting)
- [Configuration](#configuration)

### Where it lives in the code

In the `sulwe-backend` repository:

- **Service:** `services/sms_service.py`
- **Templates:** `templates/sms/`
- **Tests:** `tests/test_sms.py`

## Tea Pricing

<div class="at-a-glance" markdown>

**In short:** administrators set the price paid per kilogram of green leaf, with a start date and an optional end date. Periods may not overlap, so at any moment exactly one price applies and no delivery is ever in doubt.

**Who uses it:** administrators &nbsp;·&nbsp; **Where:** Admin dashboard → Tea pricing &nbsp;·&nbsp; **API prefix:** `/tea-prices`

</div>

### What it does

- Creates a price with **price per kilogram**, **effective from** and optional **effective to**.
- Ends a price on a given date.
- Keeps every past price, so the price history is auditable.
- Gives the [produce record](#produce-records) feature exactly one price for any date.

### Why it matters

The price is the multiplier in every payment. If the system can answer "which price applied on 12 October?" with more than one answer, two farmers with identical weights could be paid differently.

| Rule | Consequence if broken |
| --- | --- |
| One price per date | Payments would depend on query order |
| Prices are never edited after use | A farmer's amount could change after being told |
| Currency is fixed per cooperative | Mixed units would corrupt every total |

### A price record

| Field | Type | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `id` | uuid | primary key |
| `cooperative_id` | uuid | owning cooperative |
| `price_per_kg` | numeric(10,2) | amount paid for each kilogram |
| `effective_from` | date | first day the price applies |
| `effective_to` | date, nullable | last day it applies; `null` means open-ended |
| `is_active` | boolean | whether the price can still be used |
| `created_by` | uuid | who set it |

### Rules

!!! warning "No overlapping periods"
    Two active periods cannot overlap. A new price must start **after** the current one ends. Overlap answers **409 PRICE_PERIOD_OVERLAP**.

!!! success "Backdating is refused"
    A price may start today or later, but not in the past, because deliveries already recorded would need rewriting.

!!! success "Open periods close before a new one starts"
    Setting a new price while the current one is open-ended asks you to set its end date first, or does it for you in the same action: the day before the new price starts.

!!! success "Amounts are copied onto deliveries"
    Editing a price never changes an amount already recorded. A correction is a new price plus an adjustment, never an edit of history.

### How an administrator sets a price

1. Open **Tea pricing** and choose **New price**.
2. Enter the **price per kilogram**, e.g. `320`.
3. Choose **effective from** — today or a future date.
4. Leave **effective to** empty for an open-ended price.
5. Save. The system checks for overlap and refuses with a clear message if there is one.
6. From that date, every new delivery uses this price. Past deliveries keep theirs.

!!! tip "Announce the change"
    When a price changes, tell farmers through the [SMS](#sms-notifications) channel and the [USSD](#ussd-reporting) menu before the effective date. A price no one knows about causes disputes even when the calculation is right.

### Currency

All prices are in the cooperative's currency, set once at cooperative creation. The MVP fixes it to the local currency used for payouts, so the price, the delivery amount and the payment are always the same unit.

### API endpoints

| Method | Path | Who | What it does |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `GET` | `/tea-prices` | admin, agent | Lists prices, newest first |
| `POST` | `/tea-prices` | admin | Creates a price |
| `GET` | `/tea-prices/{id}` | admin | One price |
| `PATCH` | `/tea-prices/{id}` | admin | Changes price or end date |
| `POST` | `/tea-prices/{id}/deactivate` | admin | Ends a price on a date |
| `GET` | `/tea-prices/current` | agent | The price in force today |

??? example "Example: set a new price"
    ```http
    POST /tea-prices
    Authorization: Bearer <admin_token>
    Content-Type: application/json

    { "price_per_kg": 320, "effective_from": "2026-11-01" }
    ```

    ```json
    201 Created
    { "id": "9f2a...", "price_per_kg": 320,
      "effective_from": "2026-11-01", "effective_to": null, "is_active": true }
    ```

### Common errors

| Status | `code` | Cause |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 403 | `FORBIDDEN` | Agent tried to write |
| 409 | `PRICE_PERIOD_OVERLAP` | New period touches an existing one |
| 409 | `PRICE_IN_USE` | Deleting a price already applied |
| 422 | `PRICE_BACKDATED` | Start date before today |
| 422 | `PRICE_INVALID` | Zero, negative, or more than two decimals |
| 422 | `END_BEFORE_START` | End date earlier than start |

### Edge cases

- **Two price changes on the same day.** The second is refused as an overlap; end the first period and restart, or set a single new price.
- **Price change with no end date on the old price.** The system closes the old period the day before the new one starts.
- **Farmer asks about an old price.** The `GET /tea-prices` list is the history; each delivery also stores the price it used.
- **A price is entered with decimals.** Accepted to two decimals; the total is rounded once, at the end.
- **Cooperative changes currency.** Not supported in the MVP; it would invalidate history.

### Testing notes

- **API:** overlap at the boundary dates, backdating, zero, end before start.
- **UI:** the list shows a clear "current" badge on exactly one row.
- **E2E:** set price → deliver → amount matches → set new price → old delivery unchanged.

### Related

- [Produce records](#produce-records): where the price is applied.
- [Payments](#payments): what the amounts become.

### Where it lives in the code

In the `sulwe-backend` repository:

- **Router:** `routers/produce_price.py`
- **Service:** `services/produce_price.py`
- **Schema:** `schemas/produce_price.py`
- **Tests:** `tests/test_produce_price.py`

## Tasks Management

<div class="at-a-glance" markdown>

**In short:** administrators create tasks, assign them to an agent and follow them until they are done. Agents see their own tasks in the app and mark them complete.

**Who uses it:** administrators assign, agents complete &nbsp;·&nbsp; **Where:** Admin dashboard → Agent tasks, Agent app → Tasks &nbsp;·&nbsp; **API prefix:** `/tasks`

</div>

### What it does

- Creates a task with a title, description and assigned agent.
- Links a task to a collection point when it concerns one.
- Sets a priority and tracks status to completion or cancellation.
- Lets the assignee update status and add a completion note.
- Lists tasks filtered by agent, collection point or status.

### Why it matters

A cooperative runs on small jobs: check a scale, visit a farmer, collect a signature, follow up a failed payment. Without a shared list these live in someone's head or a WhatsApp thread.

| Without tasks | With tasks |
| --- | --- |
| Work is remembered, not tracked | Every job has an owner and a state |
| Nothing shows what is outstanding | Dashboard shows open work per agent |
| Handover depends on memory | The list is the handover |

### Priorities and statuses

| Priority | Use it for |
| --- | --- |
| `low` | Work that can wait |
| `normal` | Everyday work |
| `high` | Work to do first |

| Status | Meaning | Who can set it |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `open` | Assigned, not started | admin |
| `in_progress` | Being worked on | assignee, admin |
| `completed` | Done | assignee, admin |
| `cancelled` | No longer needed | admin |

### How it works

1. The administrator creates a task, chooses the agent, the priority and optionally the collection point.
2. The agent sees it under **To do**, sorted high first, then oldest.
3. The agent opens it, reads the notes, and moves it to **In progress**.
4. When finished, the agent marks it **Completed** and may add a note.
5. The administrator sees the new status on the dashboard.

### Rules

!!! success "A task belongs to one cooperative"
    The agent and the collection point must both be in the creator's cooperative. Anything else answers **404**.

!!! success "Only the assignee or an administrator moves a task"
    Another agent gets **403 FORBIDDEN**.

!!! success "Completed and cancelled are final for agents"
    An agent cannot reopen a completed task. An administrator can, which records who reopened it and when.

!!! success "Deleting is for mistakes, cancelling is for reality"
    A task with history is cancelled, not deleted. Delete answers **409 TASK_HAS_HISTORY** after any status change.

### Data stored

| Field | Type | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `id` | uuid | primary key |
| `cooperative_id` | uuid | owning cooperative |
| `title` | text | short summary |
| `description` | text | optional detail |
| `agent_id` | uuid | assignee |
| `collection_point_id` | uuid, nullable | when site-specific |
| `priority` | enum | `low`, `normal`, `high` |
| `status` | enum | `open`, `in_progress`, `completed`, `cancelled` |
| `completed_note` | text | optional |
| `due_date` | date, nullable | optional deadline |
| `created_by` | uuid | who assigned |

### API endpoints

| Method | Path | Who | What it does |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `GET` | `/tasks` | admin, agent | Lists, filtered; agents see their own |
| `POST` | `/tasks` | admin | Creates a task |
| `GET` | `/tasks/{id}` | admin, assignee | One task |
| `PATCH` | `/tasks/{id}` | admin, assignee | Details or status |
| `DELETE` | `/tasks/{id}` | admin | Removes an untouched task |

??? example "Example: create a task"
    ```http
    POST /tasks
    Authorization: Bearer <admin_token>
    Content-Type: application/json

    { "title": "Calibrate scale SLW-2026-00417",
      "description": "Reads 0.4 kg heavy against the test weight.",
      "agent_id": "8b2c...", "priority": "high", "due_date": "2026-11-12" }
    ```

    ```json
    201 Created
    { "id": "f1a9...", "status": "open", "priority": "high" }
    ```

### Common errors

| Status | `code` | Cause |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 403 | `FORBIDDEN` | Another agent touching the task |
| 404 | `TASK_NOT_FOUND` | Unknown, or another cooperative |
| 409 | `TASK_HAS_HISTORY` | Delete after a status change |
| 422 | `INVALID_STATUS_TRANSITION` | e.g. `open` straight to `completed` by an agent |

### Edge cases

- **Agent deactivated with open tasks.** Tasks stay open and are reassigned by an administrator; they do not disappear.
- **Task linked to a closed point.** Still visible, marked *point inactive*.
- **Due date in the past at creation.** Refused with **422 DUE_DATE_IN_PAST**.
- **Two administrators edit at once.** Last write wins, with `updated_at` shown.
- **Agent completes offline.** The change queues and syncs; a conflict is resolved in favour of the later timestamp, with a note on the task.

### Testing notes

- **API:** role checks, status transitions, delete with history.
- **UI:** list sorts high priority first; filters survive a refresh.
- **E2E:** admin creates → agent sees → agent completes → admin sees completed.

### Related

- [User registration](#user-registration-and-accounts)
- [Collection points](#collection-points)

### Where it lives in the code

In the `sulwe-backend` repository:

- **Router:** `routers/task.py`
- **Service:** `services/task.py`
- **Schema:** `schemas/task.py`
- **Tests:** `tests/test_tasks.py`

## Payments

<div class="at-a-glance" markdown>

**In short:** once a month an administrator pays every farmer for their accepted deliveries through M-Pesa. A payment counts as paid only when the provider confirms it, never when we ask.

**Who uses it:** cooperative administrators &nbsp;·&nbsp; **Where:** Admin dashboard → Payments &nbsp;·&nbsp; **API prefix:** `/api/payments`

</div>

### What it does

- Adds up each farmer's accepted deliveries for a month and shows what is owed.
- Creates one payment per farmer, grouped into a monthly **batch**.
- Sends each payment to the farmer's M-Pesa number through **Daraja B2C**.
- Stores the provider's answer: **paid** with a receipt, or **failed** with a reason.
- Blocks double payment of the same farmer for the same month.
- Gives the administrator a run page that follows progress to the end.

### The five steps of a monthly run

1. **Preview the month.** Totals each farmer's accepted deliveries and lists who can be paid. Farmers are excluded when they have nothing to pay, were already paid that month, or have no valid M-Pesa number.
2. **Create the batch.** The administrator confirms; one **pending** payment is created per payable farmer. Nothing is sent yet, so the run can still be abandoned.
3. **Send the payments.** Each payment is submitted once. A submitted payment can never be sent again.
4. **Receive the result.** The provider calls the webhook; the payment becomes **paid** or **failed**.
5. **Follow the run.** The run page refreshes every 5 seconds until nothing is pending.

<div class="mermaid" markdown>
flowchart LR
    A[Preview month] --> B[Create batch]
    B --> C[Send each payment]
    C --> D{Provider answer}
    D -->|success| E[paid + receipt]
    D -->|failure| F[failed + reason]
    D -->|timeout/unknown| G[uncertain]
    G --> H[Reconcile manually]
    H --> E
    H --> F
</div>

!!! tip "One payment per farmer per month"
    A farmer with a payment for that month is skipped automatically, so running the batch twice never pays twice. The database enforces this, not just the code.

### Who gets excluded, and why

| Reason | Message shown | What the administrator does |
| --- | --- | --- |
| No accepted deliveries | *Nothing to pay* | Nothing |
| Already has a payment this month | *Already paid* | Nothing |
| No phone number | *Missing phone number* | Add it on the farmer record, then re-preview |
| Invalid M-Pesa number | *Invalid number* | Correct the number, then re-preview |
| Farmer inactive | *Farmer inactive* | Reactivate or leave out |

### Payment states

Two separate things are tracked: what the farmer is **owed**, and how far the **provider request** got.

| Field | Values | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `status` | `pending`, `paid`, `failed` | The financial state |
| `submission_state` | empty, `submitted`, `accepted`, `uncertain`, `failed`, `mocked` | The transport state |

Only the provider's callback can move `status` to `paid` or `failed`. `submission_state` records how the request travelled, which matters when the answer is unclear.

### Modes

`DARAJA_SUBMISSION_MODE` decides whether requests really reach the provider.

=== "Mock (local development)"

    - **Send** records the payment as sent but never contacts Safaricom.
    - The result is simulated with **Mark paid (test)** or **Mark failed (test)**.
    - Use this mode on your own computer.

=== "Sandbox (testing)"

    - **Send** makes a real request to the sandbox. No real money moves.
    - The sandbox only pays its test number `254708374149`.
    - The result arrives through the callback to the public webhook URL.

=== "Production"

    - Refused in code for this MVP release. Any environment other than `sandbox` or `mock` raises at startup.

!!! warning "Production is switched off"
    The MVP cannot send real money. This is a deliberate guard: the flow is proven against the sandbox before anyone can move a farmer's payment.

### Safety rules

??? success "Only administrators of the same cooperative"
    Every endpoint needs a signed-in administrator and shows only their cooperative. Another cooperative's payment answers **404**.

??? success "Two keys before a sandbox payment"
    Sending needs the server setting `DARAJA_ALLOW_SANDBOX_SUBMISSIONS=true` **and** `confirm_sandbox_transfer: true` in the request body.

??? success "No manual paid or failed"
    `PATCH /api/payments/{id}/status` always answers **405 METHOD_NOT_ALLOWED**. Only the provider's callback may settle a payment.

??? success "Unclear answers block a resend"
    A timeout or an unknown answer sets `uncertain`, and the payment cannot be resent until it is reconciled. Sending twice is worse than paying late.

??? success "Protected callbacks"
    Webhooks need no login but check a secret token in the URL, compared in constant time and stripped from logs.

??? success "Batch creation is atomic"
    A batch is created in one transaction. A failure halfway leaves no partial batch.

### Data stored

| Field | Type | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `id` | uuid | primary key |
| `batch_id` | uuid | the monthly run |
| `cooperative_id` | uuid | owning cooperative |
| `farmer_id` | uuid | payee |
| `month`, `year` | int | the paid period |
| `amount` | numeric(12,0) | whole currency units |
| `status` | enum | `pending`, `paid`, `failed` |
| `submission_state` | enum | transport state |
| `provider_reference` | text | provider's id |
| `receipt_number` | text | M-Pesa receipt when paid |
| `failure_reason` | text | when failed |

Unique constraint: `(farmer_id, month, year)` — the database itself refuses a second payment.

### API endpoints

All routes start with `/api/payments`.

| Method | Path | What it does |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `POST` | `/preview` | Amount per farmer for a month; saves nothing |
| `POST` | `/batch` | Creates the month's pending payments |
| `GET` | `/batch/{batch_id}` | One batch and its payments |
| `GET` | `/?month=&year=` | Lists your cooperative's payments |
| `GET` | `/{payment_id}` | One payment |
| `GET` | `/dashboard/{cooperative_id}` | Paid, pending and failed totals |
| `POST` | `/{payment_id}/transfer` | Sends one payment to the provider |
| `POST` | `/{payment_id}/mock-result` | Mock mode only |
| `POST` | `/webhook` | Provider result callback |
| `POST` | `/webhook/timeout` | Provider timeout callback |

??? example "Example: preview a month"
    ```http
    POST /api/payments/preview
    Authorization: Bearer <admin_token>
    Content-Type: application/json

    { "month": 10, "year": 2026 }
    ```

    ```json
    200 OK
    { "total": 1846000, "payable_farmers": 62,
      "excluded": [ { "farmer": "Jean M.", "reason": "invalid_number" } ] }
    ```

### Common errors

| Status | `code` | Cause |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 403 | `FORBIDDEN` | Not an administrator |
| 404 | `PAYMENT_NOT_FOUND` | Unknown or another cooperative |
| 405 | `METHOD_NOT_ALLOWED` | Trying to set paid/failed by hand |
| 409 | `PAYMENT_ALREADY_PAID` | Farmer already paid this month |
| 409 | `PAYMENT_ALREADY_SUBMITTED` | Second send attempt |
| 409 | `PAYMENT_UNCERTAIN` | Must be reconciled first |
| 422 | `SANDBOX_CONFIRMATION_REQUIRED` | Missing `confirm_sandbox_transfer` |

### Edge cases

- **Provider timeout.** Payment becomes `uncertain`; the operator checks the provider portal, then resolves by hand. It cannot be resent meanwhile.
- **Duplicate callback.** Handled idempotently: the same provider reference is applied once.
- **Farmer changes number after the batch.** The payment keeps the number it was created with; the next month uses the new one.
- **Partial batch failure.** Payments that succeeded stay paid; the rest remain pending and are retried individually.
- **Cooperative has 600 farmers.** Sending is queued, not synchronous; the run page shows progress.
- **Month with no deliveries.** Preview returns an empty list and the batch is refused with **422 NOTHING_TO_PAY**.

### Testing notes

- **API:** double batch, double send, manual status, uncertain resend, webhook replay.
- **Integration:** mock provider returns success, failure and timeout.
- **E2E:** deliver → preview → batch → send → paid → visible by USSD.

### Related

- [Produce records](#produce-records): the deliveries payments come from.
- [USSD reporting](#ussd-reporting): how the farmer checks the payment.
- [Configuration](#configuration): Daraja settings.
- [Security](#security): access control and callbacks.

### Where it lives in the code

In the `sulwe-backend` repository:

- **Router:** `routers/payments.py`
- **Service:** `services/payment_service.py`
- **Provider:** `services/daraja_client.py`
- **Tests:** `tests/test_payment.py`

## USSD Reporting

<div class="at-a-glance" markdown>

**In short:** a farmer dials a short code on any basic phone, gets a menu in English or Kinyarwanda, and can check deliveries, payments and their own details — no smartphone, no internet, no app.

**Who uses it:** farmers &nbsp;·&nbsp; **Gateway:** Africa's Talking &nbsp;·&nbsp; **API prefix:** `/ussd`

</div>

### What it does

- Shows an interactive menu on basic mobile phones.
- Offers **English** and **Kinyarwanda**.
- Identifies the farmer by the phone number the session arrives from.
- Gives access to delivery history and payment reports.
- Refuses restricted information to numbers that are not registered farmers.
- Handles wrong choices and sessions that end without a response.

### The menu

```text
Umucyo
1. My deliveries
2. My payments
3. My details
4. Language
```

```text
My payments
1. Last payment
2. This year
0. Back
```

Every screen ends with a way back, and `0` always goes up one level, so a farmer is never trapped.

### How a session works

1. The farmer dials the cooperative's USSD code.
2. The gateway opens a session and forwards it to the backend with the caller's number.
3. The backend normalises the number and looks up the farmer.
4. If no farmer matches, it returns a short message asking them to register at their collection point, then ends.
5. If matched, it returns the menu in the farmer's stored language.
6. Each choice returns the next screen or the requested information.
7. The session ends with `END`, or when the gateway times out.

<div class="mermaid" markdown>
sequenceDiagram
    Farmer->>Gateway: dials short code
    Gateway->>API: session, phone number, input
    API->>DB: find farmer by phone
    DB-->>API: farmer + language
    API-->>Gateway: CON or END + screen
    Gateway-->>Farmer: menu on screen
</div>

### Response rules

| Rule | Reason |
| --- | --- |
| Always answer `CON` or `END` | The gateway expects exactly one of these prefixes |
| Keep screens under 182 characters | Long screens are cut by some networks |
| MSISDN is normalised before lookup | `078...`, `25078...` and `+25078...` must match the same farmer |
| No PII beyond the caller's own records | A shared phone must not expose another farmer |
| Session state stored per `sessionId` | Retries from the gateway must land on the same screen |

!!! note "Registered farmers only"
    Payment reports are only shown to numbers that belong to a registered farmer. Anyone else gets a short message and the session ends.

### What a farmer sees

| Menu item | Content |
| --- | --- |
| My deliveries | Last five deliveries: date, weight, amount, point |
| My payments | Last payment or the year's total, with month and receipt |
| My details | Name, phone, collection point |
| Language | Switch between English and Kinyarwanda |

### Why it matters

- **Inclusion:** works on any phone, including the cheapest handset.
- **No internet:** USSD runs on the signalling channel.
- **Their language:** English and Kinyarwanda, switchable from the menu.
- **Less travel:** farmers check without walking to the collection point.
- **Fewer disputes:** the same numbers the office sees are in the farmer's hand.

### API endpoints

| Method | Path | Who | What it does |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `POST` | `/ussd` | gateway | Handles one session step |
| `GET` | `/ussd/simulate` | developer | Local menu preview, mock mode only |

??? example "Example: first request of a session"
    ```http
    POST /ussd
    Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

    sessionId=ATUid_9f2a&phoneNumber=%2B250788123456&text=&serviceCode=*384*1234#
    ```

    ```text
    CON Umucyo
    1. My deliveries
    2. My payments
    3. My details
    4. Language
    ```

### Common errors

| Situation | What the farmer sees |
| --- | --- |
| Number not registered | *This number is not registered. Ask your collection point.* then `END` |
| Wrong choice | *Invalid choice. Try again.* then the menu |
| Session expired | A fresh menu on the next dial |
| Gateway retry | The same screen, not a duplicated step |

### Edge cases

- **Feature phone with a small screen.** Screens stay under 182 characters; lists show five items.
- **Farmer dials in Kinyarwanda but the record says English.** The menu still opens; language can be changed on screen 4.
- **Shared handset.** Only the caller's own records are shown, keyed by number.
- **Network retry sends the same input twice.** Session state is keyed on `sessionId` + input, so the second call returns the same screen.
- **Farmer has no deliveries yet.** An explicit *You have no deliveries this month.* rather than an empty list.
- **Very long farmer name.** Truncated on the details screen so the screen stays within limits.

### Testing notes

- **API:** unregistered number, wrong input, language switch, session replay.
- **Integration:** mock gateway posts realistic payloads with `*` and `#` encoded.
- **E2E:** dial the sandbox short code and walk the full menu.

### Related

- [SMS notifications](#sms-notifications)
- [Payments](#payments)
- [Registration](#user-registration-and-accounts)

### Where it lives in the code

In the `sulwe-backend` repository:

- **Router:** `routers/ussd.py`
- **Service:** `services/ussd_service.py`
- **Menus:** `services/ussd_menus.py`
- **Tests:** `tests/test_ussd.py`

## Development

## Backend

<div class="at-a-glance" markdown>

**In short:** the backend (`sulwe-backend`) is a FastAPI application on PostgreSQL. Each feature follows the same layers, and every rule lives in a service, never in a router.

**Read this first if** you are adding an endpoint, fixing a rule, or you keep getting a `422` when you expected a `409` — the answer is almost always "that check belongs in the service, not the schema".

</div>

### Technology

| Tool | Version | Used for | Why this one |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Python | 3.11 | The language | Type hints the whole way down; `match` and `\|` unions are usable |
| FastAPI | current | The web API | Request/response models are the documentation; the OpenAPI schema is generated, not written |
| SQLAlchemy | 2 | Database models and queries | Typed `select()` instead of string SQL, so a column rename breaks the build instead of production |
| Alembic | current | Database migrations | Every schema change is a reviewable file, not a hand-run `ALTER` |
| PostgreSQL | 15 | The database | Real constraints, partial unique indexes, and `numeric` — all three are load-bearing here |
| Pydantic | v2 | Request and response schemas | Rejects a bad payload before it reaches a service |
| uv | current | Packages and running commands | One lockfile, fast installs, same command in CI and locally |
| pytest | current | Tests | Fixtures make a seeded cooperative cheap to build per test |
| ruff | current | Linting and formatting (line length 130) | Lint and format in one tool, so there is nothing to argue about |

!!! note "One version rule"
    Python is pinned to 3.11 and everything else floats within its major version in `pyproject.toml`. The lockfile `uv.lock` is committed. If you install something new, commit the lockfile in the same commit — otherwise CI installs a different tree than you did.

### Project structure

The backend lives in [`sulwe-backend`](https://github.com/akirachix/sulwe-backend).

| Path | Holds |
| --- | --- |
| `main.py` | Creates the FastAPI app and registers the routers |
| `database.py` | Database connection and sessions |
| `middleware.py` | Request middleware |
| `core/` | Shared settings and helpers: `config.py`, `security.py`, `rate_limit.py`, `exceptions.py`, `utils.py` |
| `dependencies/permissions.py` | Role checks used by protected endpoints |
| `routers/` | Endpoints only: each one calls a single service method |
| `services/` | Business rules and validation |
| `repositories/` | Database reads and writes for each model |
| `models/` | SQLAlchemy tables, for example `farmer.py`, `produce_record.py`, `payment_transaction.py` |
| `schemas/` | Pydantic request and response shapes |
| `alembic/versions/` | Database migrations |
| `tests/` | The pytest suite |
| `Procfile` | Start command used by Heroku |
| `pyproject.toml` | Dependencies and tool settings |
| `.github/` | GitHub Actions workflows |

A request flows **router → service → repository → model → database**.

### The four layers

The same shape repeats for every feature. Learn it once and you can predict where any piece of code belongs.

| Layer | May do | May not do |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Router** | Declare the route, read the current user, call one service method, return its result | Validate a business rule, open a transaction, touch the database |
| **Service** | Apply the rules, decide which error to raise, call one or more repositories, own the transaction | Build SQL by hand, know what a `Request` object is |
| **Repository** | Read and write rows, build queries, return models | Decide whether an operation is allowed |
| **Model** | Describe the table, its columns, its constraints and its relationships | Contain business logic that depends on who is asking |

#### The router

A router contains only the route decorator, the function and **one** service call. No validation and no `try/except`.

```python
@router.post("/preview", response_model=PaymentPreviewResponse)
def preview_payments(
    payload: PaymentPreviewRequest,
    admin: CooperativeUser = Depends(get_payment_admin),
    service: PaymentService = Depends(get_payment_service),
):
    return service.preview(cooperative_user_id=admin.cooperative_user_id, month=payload.month, year=payload.year)
```

Three things to notice, because they are the review checklist:

1. `response_model` is set. The OpenAPI schema and the actual response cannot drift apart.
2. The permission check is a `Depends`, not an `if` inside the body. It appears in the generated docs as a security requirement.
3. The service is injected, not imported. That is what makes it patchable in a test.

#### The service

The service is where a feature actually lives. It receives plain values, never the request, and it raises the error the caller deserves.

```python
class PaymentService:
    def __init__(self, repo: PaymentRepository, farmers: FarmerRepository):
        self.repo = repo
        self.farmers = farmers

    def preview(self, *, cooperative_user_id: UUID, month: int, year: int) -> PaymentPreviewResponse:
        period = self.repo.find_period(month=month, year=year, cooperative_id=cooperative_id)
        if period is None:
            raise HTTPException(status_code=404, detail="No payment period for that month")
        if period.status == "committed":
            raise HTTPException(status_code=409, detail="This month is already committed")

        ready, excluded = self.farmers.split_by_readiness(month=month, year=year)
        return PaymentPreviewResponse(ready=ready, excluded=excluded, total=sum(f.net for f in ready))
```

Rules for this layer:

- **One public method per use case.** `preview`, `commit`, `send`. If you need `preview_v2`, you have a change of behaviour and it should be visible in the route.
- **Raise, do not return, failures.** A service that returns `None` for "not found" forces every caller to remember to check it; one that raises cannot be forgotten.
- **Accept keyword arguments.** `preview(cooperative_user_id=..., month=..., year=...)` cannot be called with the arguments swapped, which is the most common bug in money code.
- **No session handling.** The session comes in through the repository; the service never calls `commit()` on its own except where a use case is explicitly transactional.

#### The repository

Repositories turn questions into queries. They know the shape of the table; they know nothing about permission.

```python
class FarmerRepository:
    def __init__(self, session: Session):
        self.session = session

    def split_by_readiness(self, *, month: int, year: int):
        stmt = (
            select(ProduceRecord)
            .join(Farmer)
            .where(ProduceRecord.month == month, ProduceRecord.year == year)
            .where(ProduceRecord.price_per_kg.is_not(None))
        )
        records = self.session.scalars(stmt).all()
        ready = [r for r in records if r.farmer.momo_number]
        excluded = [r for r in records if not r.farmer.momo_number]
        return ready, excluded
```

- **Return models or plain tuples.** Not response schemas — that is the router's job.
- **One repository per aggregate.** `FarmerRepository`, not `EverythingRepository`.
- **Name methods after the question,** `find_active_by_device`, `count_unpaid_for_period`, so the query reads like the requirement.

#### The model

Models describe the table. Constraints that must hold regardless of who writes — a unique produce record per farmer per day, a non-negative weight — belong here as database constraints, not only as Python checks.

```python
class ProduceRecord(Base):
    __tablename__ = "produce_records"

    id: Mapped[UUID] = mapped_column(primary_key=True, default=uuid4)
    farmer_id: Mapped[UUID] = mapped_column(ForeignKey("farmers.id"), index=True)
    weight_kg: Mapped[Decimal] = mapped_column(Numeric(10, 2))
    price_per_kg: Mapped[Decimal | None] = mapped_column(Numeric(10, 2), nullable=True)
    recorded_at: Mapped[datetime] = mapped_column(default=func.now)

    __table_args__ = (
        CheckConstraint("weight_kg > 0", name="ck_produce_weight_positive"),
        Index("uq_produce_farmer_day", farmer_id, func.date(recorded_at), unique=True),
    )
```

The two-layer rule: **the service gives the user a clear message, the database guarantees the rule.** If you only check in Python, a concurrent request or a data fix will eventually break it.

### The life of one request

A payment preview, followed end to end.

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant C as Client
    participant M as middleware
    participant R as router
    participant P as permissions
    participant S as service
    participant Repo as repository
    participant DB as PostgreSQL

    C->>M: POST /payments/preview
    M->>M: request id, timing, rate limit
    M->>R: forwarded
    R->>P: Depends(get_payment_admin)
    P->>DB: load cooperative user and role
    P-->>R: allowed, or 403
    R->>S: preview(month, year)
    S->>Repo: find_period / split_by_readiness
    Repo->>DB: SELECT ...
    DB-->>Repo: rows
    Repo-->>S: models
    S-->>R: PaymentPreviewResponse
    R-->>C: 200 with JSON
```

Failure branches you should be able to name without looking:

| Where it fails | What the client sees | Where the code is |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Bad JSON, wrong type, missing field | `422` with `field` | Pydantic schema |
| No token, expired token | `401` | `core/security.py` |
| Wrong role for this cooperative | `403` | `dependencies/permissions.py` |
| Month not found | `404` | service |
| Already committed | `409` | service |
| Too many requests | `429` | `core/rate_limit.py` |
| Anything unhandled | `500` with a request id, never a traceback | `core/exceptions.py` |

### Conventions

!!! success "Routers stay thin"
    A router contains only the route decorator, the function and **one** service call. No validation and no `try/except`.

    ```python
    @router.post("/preview", response_model=PaymentPreviewResponse)
    def preview_payments(
        payload: PaymentPreviewRequest,
        admin: CooperativeUser = Depends(get_payment_admin),
        service: PaymentService = Depends(get_payment_service),
    ):
        return service.preview(cooperative_user_id=admin.cooperative_user_id, month=payload.month, year=payload.year)
    ```

- **Services hold the rules.** They raise HTTP errors (`404`, `409` and so on) that FastAPI returns as they are.
- **Every database change is a migration,** committed with the code that needs it.
- **Secrets come from environment variables,** never from the code.

#### Naming

| Thing | Convention | Example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Table | plural snake_case | `produce_records` |
| Model class | singular PascalCase | `ProduceRecord` |
| Route | plural noun, verb only where needed | `/farmers`, `/payments/preview` |
| Service method | the use case, imperative | `preview`, `commit`, `send_monthly_report` |
| Schema | `<Thing>Request` / `<Thing>Response` | `PaymentPreviewRequest` |
| Error code | `SCREAMING_SNAKE` in `detail.code` | `NO_PRICE_FOR_DATE` |

#### Money and weight

- Money is stored and returned as an **integer in RWF**. No floats, no cents.
- Weight is `Numeric(10, 2)`, kilograms, always positive.
- Rounding happens once, at the point of writing the payment — never in a preview and again at commit.

#### Time

- Everything is stored in UTC. The cooperative's local date is derived, never stored twice.
- A "day" for produce records is the local day, which is why the unique index uses `date(recorded_at)` rather than a stored column.

### Errors and exceptions

`core/exceptions.py` registers two handlers:

| Handler | Catches | Returns |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `http_exception_handler` | `HTTPException` raised by a service | The same body, with `code` and `field` passed through `detail` |
| `unhandled_exception_handler` | Anything else | `500`, a short message, and the request id — the traceback goes to the logs only |

Rules:

- **Never leak internals.** A `psycopg` unique-violation message must not reach the client. Catch it, map it to `409 DUPLICATE_RECORD`, log the original.
- **Always include a code.** The dashboard switches on `detail.code`, not on the English sentence.
- **Include `field` when one field is at fault,** so the form can highlight it.

### Authentication and permissions

```python
def get_payment_admin(
    user: CooperativeUser = Depends(get_current_user),
) -> CooperativeUser:
    if user.role not in ("cooperative_admin", "super_admin"):
        raise HTTPException(status_code=403, detail="Payments are for admins")
    return user
```

- Permissions live in `dependencies/permissions.py`, one function per role rule, and are attached with `Depends`.
- A dependency either returns the user or raises. It never returns a boolean.
- Routes that cross a cooperative boundary filter by `cooperative_id` from the token, never from the request body.

### Transactions

The session is opened per request and closed by a dependency. A service that writes more than one row declares its own boundary:

```python
def commit(self, *, cooperative_user_id: UUID, month: int, year: int):
    with self.session.begin():            # one transaction for the whole run
        for row in ready:
            self.repo.insert_transaction(row)
        self.repo.mark_period_committed(month, year)
```

If anything inside raises, nothing is written — which is exactly what you want when a payment run half-fails.

### Migrations

```bash
uv run alembic revision --autogenerate -m "add momo_number to farmers"
uv run alembic upgrade head
uv run alembic heads          # must print exactly one head
```

- Autogenerate is a first draft. Read the file: Alembic misses `CheckConstraint` and expression indexes, and will happily drop a column you renamed.
- Never edit a migration that has already run on staging. Add a new one.
- Destructive changes go in two releases: stop writing to the column, then drop it.

### Background work

Two things happen off the request path:

| Job | Trigger | On failure |
| --- | --- | --- |
| SMS delivery | After produce record save, after price update | Retried three times, then marked `failed` and visible on the dashboard |
| Payment run | Manual commit by an admin | Rows are per-farmer, so a single failure does not roll back the batch |

Neither uses a queue yet. Both are started from the service and recorded in a table so the outcome survives a restart — the table is the queue.

### Configuration

All settings load through `core/config.py` with Pydantic `BaseSettings`, so a missing variable stops the app at boot instead of failing at 2 a.m.

```python
class Settings(BaseSettings):
    database_url: str
    jwt_secret: str
    momo_api_key: str = ""
    momo_mock: bool = True
    model_config = SettingsConfigDict(env_file=".env")
```

`momo_mock` defaults to `True`. It must be set to `False` explicitly, in the environment, by someone who means it.

### Logging

- One line per request: method, path, status, duration, request id.
- Errors log the traceback **and** the request id.
- No phone numbers or full names in logs — `farmer_id` instead. Logs get copied into chat windows; personal data does not belong in them.

### Tests

```
tests/
├── conftest.py             # app, session, seeded cooperative, tokens
├── test_farmers.py
├── test_produce_records.py
├── test_payments.py
├── test_prices.py
└── test_permissions.py     # 403s are tested as seriously as 200s
```

- One test file per feature, mirroring `services/`.
- Secrets and the MoMo client are patched in `conftest.py`; no test makes a network call.
- Each test names the rule it protects: `test_cannot_commit_twice`, `test_price_required_before_payment`.

### Useful commands

```bash
uv run fastapi dev            # start the server
uv run pytest -q              # run the tests
uv run ruff check .           # lint
uv run ruff format .          # format
uv run alembic heads          # check there is one migration head
uv run alembic upgrade head   # apply migrations
```

| Command | When |
| --- | --- |
| `uv sync` | After pulling a change to `pyproject.toml` or `uv.lock` |
| `uv run pytest -k payments -x` | One area, stop at the first failure |
| `uv run ruff check --fix .` | Before committing |
| `uv run alembic downgrade -1` | Undo the last migration locally only |

