## Database

<div class="at-a-glance" markdown>

**In short:** everything belongs to a cooperative. Collection points, staff, farmers, prices, deliveries, tasks and payments all link back to one, directly or through a collection point.

</div>

### The shape of the data

One cooperative runs many collection points. Each collection point has agents, a scale, and the farmers who deliver there. A delivery joins a farmer, a weight, a price and the agent who recorded it. Payments group a month of deliveries for one farmer.

```mermaid
erDiagram
    COOPERATIVE ||--o{ COLLECTION_POINT : "runs"
    COOPERATIVE ||--o{ COOPERATIVE_USER : "employs"
    COOPERATIVE ||--o{ PRODUCE_PRICE : "publishes"
    COOPERATIVE ||--o{ TASK : "assigns"
    COOPERATIVE ||--o{ PAYMENT_TRANSACTION : "pays out"
    COLLECTION_POINT ||--o{ AGENT_ASSIGNMENT : "staffed by"
    COLLECTION_POINT ||--o{ FARMER : "serves"
    COLLECTION_POINT ||--o{ WEIGHING_DEVICE : "has"
    USERS ||--o| COOPERATIVE_USER : "may be staff"
    USERS ||--o| FARMER : "may be a farmer"
    FARMER ||--o{ PRODUCE_RECORD : "delivers"
    WEIGHING_DEVICE ||--o{ PRODUCE_RECORD : "weighed on"
    PRODUCE_PRICE ||--o{ PRODUCE_RECORD : "priced by"
    FARMER ||--o{ PAYMENT_TRANSACTION : "receives"
    PRODUCE_RECORD }o--|| PAYMENT_TRANSACTION : "settled in"
```

Two rules follow from that diagram and they are worth stating plainly, because they explain most of the constraints in the code:

1. **Nothing exists outside a cooperative.** Every business table carries `cooperative_id`. Every query is filtered by it. One cooperative can never see another's farmers, prices or deliveries.
2. **A delivery is the unit of truth.** Money is never stored on a farmer. A farmer's balance is always derived by summing `produce_record` rows. `payment_transaction` records the result of a payout run, not the running total.

### Main tables

| Table | Holds | Linked to |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `cooperative` | One tea cooperative | — |
| `users` | Every person: name, status and role (admin, agent, farmer) | — |
| `cooperative_user` | Staff login details: email, password hash, setup token | `users`, `cooperative`, `collection_point` |
| `collection_point` | A place where tea is weighed | `cooperative` |
| `agent_assignment` | Which agent works at which collection point | `users`, `collection_point` |
| `farmer` | A farmer and their phone number | `users`, `collection_point` |
| `weighing_device` | A scale at a collection point | `collection_point` |
| `produce_price` | Price per kilogram, with effective dates | `cooperative` |
| `produce_record` | One delivery: weight, amount, status | `farmer`, `cooperative`, `collection_point`, `weighing_device`, `produce_price`, recording agent |
| `task` | Work assigned to an agent | `cooperative`, assigned agent |
| `payment_transaction` | One farmer's monthly payment | `cooperative`, `farmer` |
| `audit_log` | A record of important actions | `users` |

#### What each table enforces

The tables above hold data. These are the rules the database itself refuses to break, independent of whatever the API happens to check.

| Table | Rule | Why |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `users` | `phone_number` is unique | The phone number is how a farmer is found on USSD. Two farmers on one number is an unresolvable support case |
| `users` | `role` is one of `admin`, `agent`, `farmer` | Roles drive every permission check; a typo becomes a security hole |
| `cooperative_user` | `email` is unique | Login identity |
| `farmer` | `cooperative_id` + `phone_number` is unique | The same number cannot belong to two farmers inside one cooperative |
| `produce_price` | No two rows for one cooperative overlap in date range | A delivery must resolve to exactly one price |
| `produce_price` | `price_per_kg > 0` | A zero price would silently pay nothing |
| `produce_record` | `weight_kg > 0` | An empty delivery is not a delivery |
| `produce_record` | `status` is one of `pending`, `processed`, `paid`, `excluded` | Payment runs filter on this column |
| `produce_record` | `amount = weight_kg * price_per_kg`, computed | Stops the stored amount drifting from the inputs |
| `weighing_device` | `device_code` is unique | One code, one physical scale |
| `payment_transaction` | `cooperative_id` + `farmer_id` + `period` is unique | A monthly run can only be committed once |
| `payment_transaction` | `status` is one of `preview`, `processing`, `paid`, `failed` | Prevents double payout |

#### The produce record, in detail

This is the table everything else is about, so its columns matter more than the rest.

| Column | Type | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `id` | uuid | Primary key |
| `cooperative_id` | uuid | Tenant filter, indexed |
| `farmer_id` | uuid | Indexed; payments group by this |
| `collection_point_id` | uuid | Where it was weighed |
| `weighing_device_id` | uuid, nullable | Null if the weight was entered by hand |
| `produce_price_id` | uuid | The price resolved on the day of delivery |
| `recorded_by` | uuid | The agent who saved it |
| `weight_kg` | numeric(10,2) | Two decimals; the scale reports grams |
| `price_per_kg` | numeric(10,2) | **Copied** from the price at the time of delivery |
| `amount` | numeric(12,2) | `weight_kg * price_per_kg`, computed |
| `delivered_on` | date | The local delivery date, not the timestamp |
| `status` | enum | `pending` on create |
| `created_at` | timestamptz | Set by the database |

Two of those columns exist for one reason each, and both are easy to get wrong:

- **`price_per_kg` is copied, not joined.** If it were read through `produce_price_id`, editing an old price would silently rewrite history and change what farmers were already paid. The copy makes every delivery an immutable financial fact.
- **`delivered_on` is a date, not a timestamp.** Payments group by delivery day in the cooperative's local time. A timestamp would put a 23:30 delivery into the wrong day.

!!! note "Money is stored as numeric, not float"
    `numeric(10,2)` for prices and `numeric(12,2)` for amounts. Floating point would produce totals that disagree by a cent after a few thousand rows, and a payment run that is one cent out is a support ticket in every cooperative.

### How the tables connect

- A **cooperative** owns collection points, staff, prices, tasks and payments.
- A **collection point** belongs to one cooperative and has agents, farmers, and scales.
- An **agent** is a user with the `agent` role, assigned to a collection point through `agent_assignment`.
- A **farmer** is a user with the `farmer` role, attached to one collection point, and identified by phone number.
- A **produce record** references the farmer, the collection point, the scale, the price that applied, and the agent who recorded it.
- A **payment transaction** references the cooperative and the farmer, and reports the totals for one period.

### Indexes that matter

| Index | Serves |
| --- | --- |
| `produce_record (cooperative_id, delivered_on)` | Payment period queries |
| `produce_record (farmer_id, delivered_on)` | A farmer's history, used by USSD |
| `produce_record (cooperative_id, status)` | The `pending` list a payment run picks up |
| `users (phone_number)` | USSD lookup and SMS sending |
| `farmer (collection_point_id)` | Collection point farmer lists |
| `produce_price (cooperative_id, effective_from, effective_to)` | Price resolution on save |
| `payment_transaction (cooperative_id, period)` | Preventing a duplicate run |

There is one expression index on `produce_price` that enforces the no-overlap rule with a `tstzrange` exclusion constraint. It is the only way to express "these date ranges must not overlap" in PostgreSQL without a trigger.

### Migrations

Every change to the database structure is an Alembic migration in `alembic/versions/`.

1. Change the model.
2. Generate the migration, then read it before applying it.
    ```bash
    uv run alembic revision --autogenerate -m "describe the change"
    ```
3. Check there is one head, back up the database, and apply it.
    ```bash
    uv run alembic heads
    uv run alembic upgrade head
    ```

!!! warning "Two heads"
    When two branches each add a migration, `alembic heads` shows two lines and `upgrade head` refuses to run. Join them with a merge migration:
    `uv run alembic merge -m "merge heads" <head1> <head2>`

!!! danger "Read autogenerated migrations"
    Autogenerate compares your models with the database. If a model is missing on your branch, it writes a `DROP TABLE` that deletes real data. Always read a migration before committing it.

#### House rules for migrations

| Rule | Reason |
| --- | --- |
| One migration per pull request | Two reviewers can read one change; nobody can review five mixed together |
| Never edit a migration that has run on staging or production | The database records the revision; editing it means your file and the database disagree |
| Name it after the change, not the date | `add_payment_period_index`, not `update_models_2` |
| Add columns as nullable, backfill, then tighten | A `NOT NULL` column added in one step fails on a table with rows |
| Drop a column two releases after it stops being used | Gives you one release to notice nothing broke |
| Test the downgrade at least once locally | A broken downgrade is discovered on the worst possible day |

#### Rollback

```bash
uv run alembic downgrade -1          # one step back
uv run alembic current               # where the database actually is
uv run alembic history --verbose     # what has run
```

Rolling back is safe for schema changes. It is **not** safe for data migrations that deleted or transformed rows — those need a backup, not a downgrade.

### Backups

| What | When | Kept |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Full logical dump | Nightly | 30 days |
| Pre-migration dump | Before every production migration | 7 days |
| Point-in-time recovery | Continuous WAL shipping | 7 days |

A pre-migration dump is taken even when the migration looks trivial. The one that looks trivial is the one that drops a column.

### Working with the database locally

```bash
uv run alembic upgrade head        # bring a fresh database to current
uv run alembic revision --autogenerate -m "change"
psql "$DATABASE_URL" -c '\dt'      # list tables
psql "$DATABASE_URL" -c '\d produce_record'   # describe one table
```

Seed data lives in `scripts/seed.py`. It creates one cooperative, one collection point, an admin, an agent, a scale and a handful of prices, so a fresh clone can record a delivery within a minute of starting.

!!! tip "Reset locally without fear"
    Local data is disposable. `uv run alembic downgrade base && uv run alembic upgrade head` gives you a clean schema in seconds. Never run `downgrade base` against any shared environment.

## Configuration

<div class="at-a-glance" markdown>

**In short:** every setting comes from an environment variable: `.env` for the backend, `.env.local` for the web apps, or the hosting platform's settings in production.

**Why it matters:** nothing in the code holds a password, a key or an address. The same build runs locally and in production; only the environment changes.

</div>

!!! danger "Never commit values"
    `.env` and `.env.local` contain passwords and keys. They are listed in `.gitignore` and must never be committed, pasted in chats or shown in screenshots.

---

### How configuration is loaded

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    A[".env file<br/>local"] --> C
    B["Hosting platform<br/>settings"] --> C
    C["Pydantic Settings<br/>validated at startup"] --> D["Config object<br/>used by services"]
    C -.->|missing or invalid| E["App refuses to start"]
```

Four rules follow from this.

1. **Read once, at startup.** Settings are parsed into a typed object when the process boots. Code never calls `os.getenv` deep inside a service.
2. **Fail fast.** A missing `SECRET_KEY` or a malformed `DATABASE_URL` stops the app with a clear message, instead of failing hours later on a farmer's first login.
3. **Defaults are for development only.** Every default in the table below is a safe local value. Production must set the real one.
4. **Nothing secret reaches the browser.** Only variables prefixed `NEXT_PUBLIC_` are exposed to client code in the Next.js apps. Everything else stays on the server.

---

### The three files

| File | Used by | Committed? | Purpose |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `.env.example` | Everyone | **Yes** | The list of variables with placeholder values. Copied to `.env` |
| `.env` | FastAPI backend | No | Real local values |
| `.env.local` | Admin dashboard, agent app | No | Real local values, one file per app |
| `.env.test` | Test suite | Yes | Throwaway values, a test database URL, mocked SMS and payments |

!!! tip "Starting locally"
    ```bash
    cp .env.example .env          # backend
    cp .env.example .env.local    # each Next.js app
    ```
    Then fill in the values. If a variable is new, add it to `.env.example` in the same pull request that uses it, otherwise the next developer hits a confusing startup error.

---

### Backend

=== "App and database"

    | Variable | Purpose | Required | Local default |
    | --- | --- | --- | --- |
    | `DATABASE_URL` | PostgreSQL connection | Yes | `postgresql://localhost:5432/umucyo` |
    | `APP_NAME` | Shown in API docs and log lines | No | `Umucyo` |
    | `APP_ENV` | `local`, `staging` or `production` | No | `local` |
    | `DEBUG` | Verbose errors and SQL logging | No | `true` locally, `false` always in production |
    | `FRONTEND_URL` | Base address used in links inside emails | Yes | `http://localhost:3000` |
    | `CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS` | Web app addresses allowed to call the API, comma-separated | Yes | `http://localhost:3000,http://localhost:3001` |
    | `BOOTSTRAP_SECRET` | Secret needed to create the first administrator | Yes, first run only | Random string |

    !!! warning "`DEBUG=true` in production"
        It returns stack traces and SQL to the caller. Set it to `false` and confirm the value on the hosting platform, not just in your local file.

    `CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS` must list exact origins, including the scheme and port. A wildcard `*` is refused when credentials are allowed, which is why it is a list rather than a flag.

=== "Login and email"

    | Variable | Purpose | Required | Local default |
    | --- | --- | --- | --- |
    | `SECRET_KEY` | Signs login tokens | Yes | Any long random string |
    | `JWT_SECRET_KEY`, `JWT_ALGORITHM` | Optional token overrides | No | Falls back to `SECRET_KEY`, `HS256` |
    | `ACCESS_TOKEN_EXPIRE_MINUTES` | Token lifetime | No | `30` |
    | `OTP_EXPIRE_MINUTES` | Login code lifetime | No | `10` |
    | `PASSWORD_SETUP_EXPIRE_HOURS` | Agent invitation lifetime | No | `24` |
    | `SMTP_HOST`, `SMTP_PORT` | Email server | No | Gmail, `587` |
    | `SMTP_EMAIL` | Account that authenticates | No | — |
    | `SMTP_FROM_EMAIL` | Address shown to recipients | No | Same as `SMTP_EMAIL` |
    | `SMTP_PASSWORD` | Sending account password; for Gmail, an app password | No | — |

    **`SECRET_KEY` is the one that must never change casually.** Rotating it signs every user out instantly, because existing tokens no longer verify. Rotate it only when you believe it has leaked, and expect the logout.

    Generate a strong value:

    ```bash
    python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(48))"
    ```

    !!! note "Gmail app passwords"
        A normal Gmail password will not work once two-step verification is on. Create an app password in the Google account settings and use that as `SMTP_PASSWORD`.

=== "SMS"

    | Variable | Purpose | Required |
    | --- | --- | --- |
    | `SMS_LEOPARD_API_KEY` | SMS Leopard key | Yes |
    | `SMS_LEOPARD_API_SECRET` | SMS Leopard secret | Yes |
    | `SMS_LEOPARD_SENDER_ID` | Sender name farmers see | Yes |

    The sender ID is what appears on a farmer's handset. It should match the name the cooperative uses in the field, so a message is not mistaken for a scam. Use the same ID in staging so test messages look identical to real ones.

    When the credentials are absent in local development, SMS sending is skipped and the message is written to the log instead of being charged for.

=== "Payments"

    | Variable | Purpose | Local default |
    | --- | --- | --- |
    | `PAYMENT_CURRENCY`, `DARAJA_CURRENCY` | Both `KES` | `KES` |
    | `DARAJA_ENVIRONMENT` | `sandbox` (production is refused) | `sandbox` |
    | `DARAJA_SUBMISSION_MODE` | `mock` or `sandbox` | `mock` |
    | `DARAJA_ALLOW_SANDBOX_SUBMISSIONS` | `true` to allow sandbox sends | `false` |
    | `DARAJA_CONSUMER_KEY`, `DARAJA_CONSUMER_SECRET` | Daraja app credentials | — |
    | `DARAJA_SHORTCODE` | B2C short code | — |
    | `DARAJA_INITIATOR_NAME`, `DARAJA_INITIATOR_PASSWORD` | B2C account details | — |
    | `DARAJA_SECURITY_CERTIFICATE` | Safaricom's certificate | — |
    | `DARAJA_CALLBACK_TOKEN` | Secret in the callback URLs (32+ characters) | Random string |
    | `DARAJA_RESULT_URL`, `DARAJA_TIMEOUT_URL` | Public HTTPS webhook addresses ending in `?token=` | — |

    **Two switches protect real money.**

    - `DARAJA_SUBMISSION_MODE=mock` never calls the provider. Use it locally and in tests.
    - `DARAJA_ALLOW_SANDBOX_SUBMISSIONS=false` blocks even sandbox sends. Leave it `false` unless you are deliberately testing the request path.

    The callback token is checked on every webhook. A request without the right `?token=` is rejected before any record changes, which is what stops a stranger posting a fake "paid" result.

    ```bash
    python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(32))"
    ```

    !!! warning "Currency is a deployment decision, not a code change"
        These variables are named for Daraja and default to `KES`. If a deployment moves to another market and provider, this is the whole list to review — currency names, credentials, callback paths and the security certificate — plus the currency label shown in the dashboard. Change them as a set, never one at a time.

---

### Admin dashboard

| Variable | Purpose | Example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL` | Backend address | `https://your-backend` |
| `NEXT_PUBLIC_PAYMENTS_API_URL` | Payments API, the backend address plus `/api/payments` | `https://your-backend/api/payments` |
| `NEXT_PUBLIC_AGENT_APP_URL` | Agent app address, where agents are sent after login | `https://your-agent-app` |

!!! tip "Changing a `NEXT_PUBLIC_` value"
    These values are built into the app. Restart `npm run dev` locally, and redeploy the hosted app after changing them.

Because they are compiled in, a `NEXT_PUBLIC_` variable is **not secret**. Anything put there is readable by anyone who opens the browser's developer tools. Keys belong in the backend.

---

### Agent app

| Variable | Purpose | Example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL` | Backend address | `https://your-backend` |

The agent app talks to the same API as the dashboard. It has no payments configuration of its own; payments are triggered from the dashboard and recorded by the backend.

---

### Per-environment checklist

| Setting | Local | Staging | Production |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `DEBUG` | `true` | `false` | `false` |
| `SECRET_KEY` | Any string | Long random, different from production | Long random, stored in the platform's secret store |
| `DATABASE_URL` | Local Postgres | Staging database | Production database, backups on |
| `DARAJA_SUBMISSION_MODE` | `mock` | `sandbox` | `sandbox` until the paid pilot is signed off |
| `DARAJA_ALLOW_SANDBOX_SUBMISSIONS` | `false` | `false` | Reviewed case by case |
| SMS credentials | Absent, messages logged | Test credentials | Live credentials |
| `CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS` | Localhost ports | Staging domains | Only the live domains |

---

### Rotating a secret

1. Create the new value and store it in the platform's secret store.
2. Deploy with **both** values accepted, where the service supports it.
3. Confirm logins, SMS and webhooks still work.
4. Remove the old value and deploy again.
5. Record what changed and when.

For `SECRET_KEY` there is no overlap window: a rotation signs everyone out. Do it during a quiet period and tell the cooperative's administrators first.

---

### When configuration is wrong

| Symptom | Cause | Fix |
| --- | --- | --- |
| App exits at boot with a validation error | A required variable is missing or empty | Compare `.env` against `.env.example` |
| Every request returns a CORS error | Origin missing from `CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS`, or wrong port or scheme | Add the exact origin, restart |
| Login works, then drops after 30 minutes | `ACCESS_TOKEN_EXPIRE_MINUTES` too short for field use | Raise it, keeping the security trade in mind |
| Emails never arrive | Wrong `SMTP_PORT`, or a normal Gmail password instead of an app password | Use `587` and an app password |
| Farmers get no SMS | Missing SMS credentials, so sending is skipped and logged | Set the three SMS variables |
| Payment callbacks rejected | `DARAJA_CALLBACK_TOKEN` differs between backend and provider URL | Make both identical, confirm the `?token=` value |
| Agents cannot log in from the dashboard | `NEXT_PUBLIC_AGENT_APP_URL` missing or not redeployed | Fix the value, redeploy |
| Dashboard calls the wrong API | `NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL` changed but the app was not rebuilt | Restart or redeploy |

---

### House rules

- Every new variable is added to `.env.example` with a placeholder and a one-line comment, in the same pull request.
- No secret is ever typed into a ticket, a chat or a commit. Share via the platform's secret store.
- Production values are set on the hosting platform, never by uploading a file.
- A variable that is no longer read is removed from `.env.example` in the same pull request that stops reading it.
- Live SMS and live payment credentials never appear on staging.

## Admin Dashboard

<div class="at-a-glance" markdown>

**In short:** the admin dashboard (`sulwe-dashboard`) is the web app cooperative administrators use to run the cooperative.

**Built with:** Next.js (App Router), React, Tailwind CSS, TanStack React Query

**Live:** [sulwe-dashboard.vercel.app](https://sulwe-dashboard.vercel.app/){ target="_blank" }

</div>

### Dashboard home

![Umucyo admin dashboard home](assets/brand/dashboard.png)

*The home page gives administrators an overview of their cooperative. Click the image to enlarge it.*

[:material-open-in-new: Open the live dashboard](https://sulwe-dashboard.vercel.app/){ .md-button .md-button--primary target="_blank" }

### What the dashboard is for

The dashboard is the office side of Umucyo. Farmers and collection agents never use it — they work through USSD and SMS. The dashboard is where the cooperative's staff do the four jobs that need a screen and a keyboard:

| Job | Where it happens | How often |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Registering farmers and agents | Farmers, Agents | Daily, at intake |
| Registering weighing devices and watching them | Weighing devices | At setup, then weekly |
| Setting the tea price and running payments | Tea pricing, Payments | Monthly cycle |
| Reading reports and fixing day-to-day problems | Reports, Deliveries | Daily |

Everything the dashboard does is a call to the backend in [Backend](#backend). The dashboard stores no business data of its own, with one deliberate exception: the sign-in token, which lives in the browser so the administrator does not have to sign in on every page.

!!! note "The dashboard is not the source of truth"
    If a farmer says "my delivery is missing", the answer comes from the database, not from what the screen last showed. That is why every page has a refresh (see [Refreshing data](#refreshing-data)) and why the dashboard never edits a delivery's weight.

### Pages

| Page | Address |
| --- | --- |
| Landing | `/landing` |
| Sign in, Sign up | `/signin`, `/signup` |
| Login code | `/mfa` |
| Forgot password, Set password | `/forgot-password`, `/set-password` |
| Home | `/dashboard/home` |
| Farmers | `/dashboard/farmer` |
| Agents, Register an agent | `/dashboard/agent`, `/dashboard/register_agent` |
| Agent tasks | `/dashboard/agent-tasks` |
| Deliveries | `/dashboard/deliveries` |
| Weighing devices | `/dashboard/weighing-device` |
| Tea pricing | `/dashboard/tea-pricing` |
| Payments | `/dashboard/payments` |
| Reports | `/dashboard/reports` |
| Settings | `/dashboard/settings` |

### What each page does

#### Signing in

`/signin` asks for an email address and password, then sends a one-time login code to that address. `/mfa` takes the code. The code is short-lived, so the page has a resend action with a short cooldown; the cooldown exists to stop an impatient administrator from burning through the provider's sending limit.

`/forgot-password` starts a reset, and `/set-password` finishes it. **Keep me logged in** on the sign-in page decides whether the token survives a browser restart.

!!! warning "One session per browser"
    Signing in on a second device does not sign out the first. If an administrator leaves a shared office machine signed in, sign out with the button in Settings rather than closing the tab — closing the tab keeps the token.

#### Home

The landing page after sign-in. It shows the cooperative at a glance: recent deliveries, the current tea price, and anything that needs attention — a weighing device gone quiet, or a payment run that failed. It is a read-only page; every number links through to the page that owns it.

#### Farmers and Agents

`/dashboard/farmer` lists farmers with search by name and phone number. A farmer record carries the collection point they belong to, which decides where their deliveries are expected to come from.

`/dashboard/agent` lists collection agents; `/dashboard/register_agent` creates one. Agents are the people who run weighing at a collection point, so registering one is really two steps: create the account, then attach it to a point.

| Field | Notes |
| --- | --- |
| Full name | As it appears on the cooperative register |
| Phone number | The number the USSD session and SMS use — one number, one farmer |
| Collection point | Decides where the agent works and which deliveries they see |
| Status | Active or inactive; an inactive agent cannot be assigned tasks |

#### Agent tasks

`/dashboard/agent-tasks` shows work assigned to agents and its state — assigned, in progress, done. This is the screen that answers "has anyone weighed at that point today". Tasks also appear on the agent side as a list in the agent app; the dashboard only reads and assigns.

#### Deliveries

`/dashboard/deliveries` is the ledger of what farmers brought to collection points. Each row comes from a weighing, so the weight shown is what the device recorded — a person cannot correct it here. Where a weight is wrong, the fix is at the device (re-tare and re-weigh), which leaves an audit trail instead of a silent edit.

The page filters by date, collection point and farmer, and shows the price that applied on the delivery date, not today's price. That distinction matters: a delivery made on the 3rd is paid at the 3rd's price even if the price changed on the 5th.

#### Weighing devices

`/dashboard/weighing-device` lists registered devices with their last-seen time and status. A device that has not reported for a while is shown as **stale** rather than **offline**, because the dashboard only knows what the backend last heard.

Registering a device is a one-time pairing step. The device is identified by its serial number, and the pairing record is what lets the backend accept readings from it and reject everything else.

#### Tea pricing

`/dashboard/tea-pricing` sets the price per kilogram for a period. The price is not a single number — it is a period with a start and an end date. Every delivery looks up the price that covers its date.

!!! danger "A price change does not rewrite history"
    Setting a new price from the 15th leaves deliveries before the 15th at the old price. If you set a price that overlaps an existing period, the backend refuses it. That refusal is deliberate — overlapping prices make "how much do we owe?" unanswerable.

If a price was entered wrongly, correct it in the same period rather than starting a new one, and only while no payment has been paid against it. Once a payment run has completed, the price is frozen.

#### Payments

`/dashboard/payments` runs the monthly payment cycle. It is a **preview-then-commit** flow:

1. Choose a period and preview the run.
2. Read the preview: who will be paid, how much, and who is excluded.
3. Confirm, and the run is sent to the payment provider.

The preview is the whole point. It is the last moment a wrong price or a missing phone number is cheap to fix. Nothing is sent until the administrator confirms, and a completed run cannot be re-run for the same period — that protection is what stops a double payment.

| Button | What it does | Safe to click twice? |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Preview | Builds the run from deliveries and prices | Yes — reads only |
| Confirm | Sends the run to the provider | No — the backend rejects a repeat for the same period |
| Refresh status | Re-reads the provider's view of the run | Yes |

#### Reports

`/dashboard/reports` turns deliveries and payments into the numbers the cooperative reports upward: kilos per collection point, per farmer, per month, and the money that went out. Reports are read-only and always derived from stored records, never typed in.

#### Settings

`/dashboard/settings` holds the cooperative's own details and the signed-in administrator's account: name, contact details, and the sign-out action.

### Code layout

The dashboard lives in [`sulwe-dashboard`](https://github.com/akirachix/sulwe-dashboard).

| Path | Holds |
| --- | --- |
| `app/` | Routes: each folder with a `page.jsx` is one page |
| `app/dashboard/` | The signed-in pages listed above |
| `app/app-shell.jsx`, `app/layout.jsx` | The shared layout |
| `components/` | Shared parts: `payments/`, `reports/`, `sidebar/` |
| `components/Providers.jsx` | App-wide providers, such as React Query |
| `lib/api.js`, `lib/auth.ts` | Backend calls and sign-in helpers |
| `lib/payments/` | Payment API calls (`api.ts`), types and period helpers |
| `lib/reportsApi.ts`, `lib/reportTypes.ts`, `lib/reportLabels.ts` | Reports data |
| `lib/currency.ts` | Money formatting |
| `.husky/` | Pre-commit hook that runs ESLint and Prettier |
| `.github/` | GitHub Actions workflows |

#### Where a change goes

| You want to… | Touch |
| --- | --- |
| Add a page | `app/dashboard/<name>/page.jsx` |
| Change a table or a form | `components/` (share it) or the page's own folder (keep it local) |
| Add a backend call | `lib/api.js`, or a named module in `lib/` if it has its own types |
| Change how money is shown | `lib/currency.ts` — never format money inline on a page |
| Change the layout or the sidebar | `app/app-shell.jsx`, `components/sidebar/` |
| Add a query or a mutation | The page, using the shared React Query client from `components/Providers.jsx` |

Two habits keep this listing honest. First, a component used by one page stays in that page's folder until a second page needs it — then it moves to `components/`. Second, nothing in `components/` imports from `app/dashboard/`, so the shared parts never depend on one screen.

### How it talks to the backend

- Every request goes through `lib/`, which adds the backend address and the `Authorization: Bearer` token.
- The token is kept in `localStorage` when **Keep me logged in** is selected.

#### The request path

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    A["Page (app/dashboard/...)"] --> B["React Query hook"]
    B --> C["lib/api.js or lib/payments/api.ts"]
    C --> D["Auth header + base URL"]
    D --> E["Backend"]
    E --> F["Envelope: data or detail"]
    F --> B
    B --> G["Loading / error / data state"]
```

The rules that come out of that path:

1. **Pages never call `fetch` directly.** Every call goes through `lib/`, so the base URL and the token exist in one place. A page that builds its own URL will work locally and fail in production.
2. **A missing token means a redirect, not a blank page.** The guard sends the administrator to `/signin` and remembers where they were going.
3. **A `401` clears the token and signs out.** A `403` does not — it means "signed in, not allowed", and showing the sign-in page there would be confusing.
4. **Errors are shown, not swallowed.** Every query renders three states: loading, error with a retry, and data. An empty table and a failed request must never look the same.

#### The token

| Question | Answer |
| --- | --- |
| Where is it stored? | `localStorage`, only when **Keep me logged in** is ticked |
| What is sent? | `Authorization: Bearer <token>` on every call |
| When does it expire? | Per the backend's token lifetime; an expired token returns `401` |
| What happens on sign out? | The token is removed and the cached queries are cleared |

!!! warning "`localStorage` is readable by any script on the page"
    That is acceptable for an internal admin tool with no third-party scripts, and it is written down here so nobody adds one later without thinking about it. Session storage would shrink the window but would also sign people out on every new tab.

#### Refreshing data

React Query caches each query under a key — `deliveries`, with the filters included. Two consequences worth knowing:

- Navigating back to a page shows the cached result first, then refreshes. The screen is never blank while it re-fetches.
- After a mutation that changes records (confirming a payment, adding a farmer), the matching query is invalidated so the list re-reads. If a list looks stale after an action, the mutation is missing its invalidation, not the backend.

#### Money and dates

Money arrives from the backend as an integer in the smallest unit. It is formatted for display in `lib/currency.ts` only, and shown in the cooperative's currency. Never do arithmetic in the view layer — the backend's numbers are the payable ones, and a client-side total that disagrees with a payment run only causes an argument.

Dates are stored in UTC and shown in the browser's time zone. A delivery dated "today" is resolved on the backend against the cooperative's local day, so an administrator travelling does not shift what counts as today.

### Building locally

```bash
git clone https://github.com/akirachix/sulwe-dashboard.git
cd sulwe-dashboard
npm install
cp .env.example .env.local        # then set the backend address
npm run dev
```

| Variable | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| Backend base URL | Where `lib/api.js` sends requests |
| Any `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` value | Baked into the browser bundle at build time — changing one needs a restart of `npm run dev` and a rebuild on Vercel |

The app runs on `http://localhost:3000` by default. Point it at a local backend or at staging — never at production with live payment credentials.

### When something looks wrong

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Every page bounces to `/signin` | Token missing or expired | Sign in again; check the `Authorization` header in the network tab |
| Pages work locally, 404 in production | Backend URL hard-coded in a page instead of `lib/` | Route the call through `lib/api.js` |
| `401` on every call after a deploy | Token signed with an old `SECRET_KEY` | Sign in again; see [Configuration](#configuration) |
| Empty table with no message | The error state is missing | Add the error branch; an empty list and a failure must not look alike |
| Money shows one decimal place | Formatted inline instead of `lib/currency.ts` | Use the shared formatter |
| Dashboard shows yesterday's payment status | Query cache not invalidated | Invalidate the payments query after confirm, or press refresh |
| Prices refuse to save | The period overlaps an existing one | Change the dates — see [Tea pricing](#tea-pricing) |

### A short checklist before a pull request

- [ ] Every backend call goes through `lib/`
- [ ] Loading, error and empty states all render
- [ ] Money uses `lib/currency.ts`; dates render in the browser's zone
- [ ] A `401` signs out, a `403` shows a permission message
- [ ] New pages are added to the sidebar and to the pages table above
- [ ] `npm run lint` and `npm run format` are clean — the pre-commit hook will run them anyway
- [ ] No secrets in `NEXT_PUBLIC_*`

## Agent App (PWA)

<div class="at-a-glance" markdown>

**In short:** the agent app (`sulwe-frontend`) is the installable web app agents use at collection points to register farmers, record deliveries and complete tasks.

**Built with:** Next.js (App Router), TypeScript, TanStack React Query

**Who uses it:** collection agents only. Farmers use USSD and SMS; administrators use the dashboard.

**Where it runs:** any modern phone browser, or installed to the home screen, pointed at `https://sulwe-agent.vercel.app`.

</div>

### What agents do in it

- See today's summary: kilograms collected, tasks done and deliveries.
- Register new farmers.
- Record new deliveries on the collection point's scale.
- Work through assigned tasks and mark them done.

Every action in the app is scoped to **one collection point** — the one the signed-in agent belongs to. An agent cannot see or touch another point's farmers, deliveries or tasks, and the backend enforces that even if the app asked for it.

### Why it is a PWA and not a native app

| Reason | What it buys us |
| --- | --- |
| One codebase | The same TypeScript skills maintain the dashboard and the agent app |
| No store review | A fix can be live in minutes, not in days |
| Always the current version | The agent has no old build to leave uninstalled |
| Installable | "Add to Home Screen" gives an icon and a full-screen window, so it feels like an app |
| Shareable login | The dashboard signs the agent in and hands over the token; no second account |

What a PWA does **not** give us: background work when the app is closed, reliable barcode or Bluetooth access on every handset, or a guaranteed offline cache. Those are the trades, and they are why the next section exists.

### Pages

| Page | Route | What agents do there |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Home | `/` | Today's totals and recent deliveries |
| Tasks | `/tasks` | To do and done tasks, with search |
| Task details | `/tasks/[id]` | Read a task's notes and mark it done |
| Register farmer | `/farmers/new` | Add a farmer to their collection point |
| New delivery | `/deliveries/new` | Record a farmer's delivery |

All agent pages share one layout: a header with the collection point's name, a sidebar with the four main destinations, and a sign-out control. There is no page in this app for prices, payments or reports — those belong to the dashboard, and hiding them from agents is a deliberate scope decision, not an oversight.

### Home

The home screen answers one question: *what is happening at my collection point today?*

| Block | Shows | Refreshes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Today's kilograms | Sum of accepted deliveries since local midnight | On focus, and every 60 seconds |
| Today's deliveries | Count, accepted and rejected | Same |
| Tasks finished today | Count of tasks marked done today | Same |
| Recent deliveries | The last 10, newest first, with farmer name, weight, status and whether the SMS was sent | On focus |

"Today" means the local day at the cooperative's timezone, not UTC. A delivery recorded at 00:30 local time belongs to the new day even though its UTC timestamp is the previous date. The backend computes the boundary; the app only displays it.

### Tasks

Tasks are created by an administrator in the dashboard and assigned to an agent. The agent app shows only their own tasks, split into **To do** and **Done**.

- **To do** — ordered oldest first, because the oldest is the one most likely to be overdue.
- **Done** — newest first, so the last thing finished is at the top.
- **Search** — matches the task title and its notes, client-side, case-insensitive.
- **Marking done** — opens the task details, then a confirm button. The screen stays open until the server confirms; if the request fails the task stays in **To do** and an error is shown.

There is no "unassign" or "delete" in this app. An agent finishing work is the only transition here; moving a task to another agent is the administrator's job.

### Task details

Shows the title, the notes written by the administrator, when it was created and, once done, when it was completed. The notes field is read-only — agents report anything worth recording through the delivery note or by telling the administrator, not by editing the task.

### Register farmer

| Field | Required | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| First name | Yes | Trimmed; 2–60 characters |
| Last name | Yes | Trimmed; 2–60 characters |
| Phone number | Yes | Rwandan format, stored normalised (`+2507XXXXXXXX`) |
| Collection point | Yes | Pre-filled with the agent's own point, editable only if the agent belongs to more than one |
| Agrees to SMS | Yes | Must be ticked; the farmer is told they will get an SMS after every delivery |

On success the farmer appears immediately in the delivery lookup. A duplicate phone number at the same collection point is refused with a clear message; the same phone number at a *different* point is allowed, because a farmer may legitimately sell at two sheds.

!!! warning "The SMS consent tick is not decoration"
    Unticking it is the only way a farmer is excluded from delivery SMS. Record it honestly — an agent ticking it on a farmer's behalf without asking is the one mistake in this screen with a legal consequence.

### New delivery

This is the screen the whole system exists for. A delivery is recorded in three steps, and each step corresponds to a real object at the shed.

1. **Find the farmer** by name or phone number. Results appear as the agent types; a farmer must exist before a delivery can be recorded.
2. **Read the weight.** With a connected scale, the stable reading appears automatically and the agent can re-weigh. If the scale is not working, the weight can be entered by hand.
3. **Choose the status**, Accepted or Rejected, add an optional note, then press **Save and send SMS**. The farmer receives the weight and result by SMS.

| Step | What the app sends | What can go wrong |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Find farmer | `GET /farmers?search=…` | No match — offer to register, don't force a fuzzy pick |
| Read weight | Nothing; the reading comes from the scale | No scale — manual entry, flagged in the record |
| Save | `POST /deliveries` with an `Idempotency-Key` | Timeout on a slow network — safe to retry, the key prevents a double record |

**The app never sends a price.** Price is resolved on the backend from the period covering the delivery date. That separation is what lets a price be corrected retroactively without touching a single delivery.

#### The idempotency key

Before the save request, the app generates a key (a UUID) and holds it until the server answers. If the request times out and the agent presses save again, the same key goes with it and the backend returns the original delivery instead of creating a second one. This matters most on the poor connections collection points actually have.

#### After saving

The screen closes and the agent lands back on the home summary, where the new delivery appears at the top of recent deliveries. The SMS status column shows **sent**, **pending** or **failed**:

| SMS status | Meaning | What the agent does |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Sent | The provider accepted the message | Nothing |
| Pending | Queued, provider not answered yet | Nothing yet; it updates on refresh |
| Failed | The provider rejected it or the number is unreachable | Tell the administrator; the delivery still counts |

A failed SMS never rolls back a delivery. The weight is the record; the message is a notification about it.

### Internet connection

The agent app needs an internet connection to save records. Offline saving, where records are kept on the phone and sent when the connection returns, is planned but not built yet.

Until then, the app behaves like this:

- **Reading** — the last fetched list stays on screen while the connection is down, but it may be stale, and nothing marks it as such beyond the request failing on the next refresh.
- **Saving** — the request fails and the form keeps its values, so the agent can retry without re-entering anything.
- **Signing in** — impossible while offline; the token cannot be refreshed.

!!! note "Why offline is the next real feature"
    Collection points are rural and coverage is intermittent. A queue in the browser (IndexedDB) with a replay on reconnect would make the app usable through a full outage. The `Idempotency-Key` already in the save path is the piece that makes replay safe — the reason it was built first.

### Code layout

| Folder | Holds |
| --- | --- |
| `app/` | Routes only; agent pages share one layout with the sidebar |
| `components/<feature>/` | Screens and their parts, for example `components/tasks/` |
| `components/providers/` | App-wide providers, such as React Query |
| `lib/` | Backend calls, the API helper and shared types |

The rule that keeps this workable: **`app/` holds routing, not logic.** A route file renders a component from `components/<feature>/` and passes it params. Anything longer than a dozen lines of JSX in a route file is a sign it belongs in a component.

| If you are changing… | Open |
| --- | --- |
| How a delivery is saved | `components/deliveries/` and `lib/api/deliveries.ts` |
| What the home summary shows | `components/home/` |
| The task list sort order | `components/tasks/` |
| The token, 401 handling, or base URL | `lib/api/client.ts` |
| Field labels or validation messages | `components/<feature>/` — never in `app/` |

### Signing in

Agents sign in on the same login as administrators. After login, the dashboard sends agents to this app with their token.

The flow, in order:

1. The agent opens the dashboard and enters email, password, then the 6-digit code sent by email.
2. The backend returns a token whose payload says the user's role.
3. The dashboard sees `role: agent` and redirects to the agent app with the token.
4. The agent app stores the token and adds `Authorization: Bearer <token>` to every request.
5. A `401` anywhere clears the token and returns to sign-in; a `403` shows "not allowed" and stays put.

| Token detail | Value |
| --- | --- |
| Lifetime | 24 hours |
| Refresh | None; sign in again when it expires |
| Storage | Browser storage, readable by the app |
| Signed with | `SECRET_KEY` on the backend |

!!! note "Design and current behaviour"
    The design allows signing in with a phone number or email. The current backend login (`POST /auth/login`) accepts **email** and password.

Changing `SECRET_KEY` invalidates every token at once. That is expected, not a bug: every agent is signed out and must sign in again.

### Screen designs

These are the Figma designs for the agent app. The live app follows them; small details may differ. Click any image to enlarge it.

#### Sign in

![Sign in screen design](assets/design/signin.png)

The agent signs in, then enters the 6-digit code sent by email.

!!! note "Design and current behaviour"
    The design allows signing in with a phone number or email. The current backend login (`POST /auth/login`) accepts **email** and password.

#### Home

![Agent home screen design](assets/design/agent-home.png){ width="300" }

Shows the agent's collection point, the kilograms collected today, today's deliveries and finished tasks, and recent deliveries with their status (Accepted or Rejected) and whether the SMS was sent.

#### Tasks

![Tasks screen design](assets/design/agent-tasks.png){ width="300" }

Tasks assigned by the administrator, split into **To do** and **Done**, with a search box.

#### New delivery

![New delivery steps: find farmer, read weight, choose status](assets/design/new-delivery.png)

Recording a delivery takes three steps:

1. **Find the farmer** by name or phone number.
2. **Read the weight.** With a connected scale, the stable reading appears automatically and the agent can re-weigh. If the scale is not working, the weight can be entered by hand.
3. **Choose the status**, Accepted or Rejected, add an optional note, then press **Save and send SMS**. The farmer receives the weight and result by SMS.

#### Register a farmer

![Register farmer screen design](assets/design/register-farmer.png){ width="300" }

The agent enters the farmer's first name, last name, phone number and collection point, and confirms the farmer agrees to receive SMS reports.

### When something looks wrong

| What the agent sees | Likely cause | What to do |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Signed out on every tap | Token expired, or `SECRET_KEY` changed | Sign in again; if it repeats hourly, check the backend clock |
| "Not allowed" on one page only | Role or collection point mismatch | Confirm the agent is assigned to the right point in the dashboard |
| Table is empty but no error | Genuinely no records for that filter | Try clearing the search; check the point name in the header |
| Save spins forever | Weak connection | Wait for the timeout, then press save again — the idempotency key makes it safe |
| Delivery saved, SMS "failed" | Provider rejected the number | The delivery stands; report the number to the administrator |
| Weight fields greyed out | No scale detected | Use manual entry; the record is marked as manual |
| Old data after an edit elsewhere | Query cache | Pull to refresh, or reload the app |

### Testing on a phone

```bash
cd ~/sulwe-frontend
pnpm install
cp .env.example .env.local     
pnpm dev --hostname 0.0.0.0 --port 3000
```

Then open `http://<your-laptop-ip>:3000` on a phone on the same Wi-Fi. `--hostname 0.0.0.0` is the part people forget; without it the dev server only answers on `localhost`.

To try install behaviour, open the site in Chrome on Android or Safari on iOS and use **Add to Home Screen**. Test the full-screen window, not just the browser tab — safe-area insets on notched phones are the thing that usually needs fixing.

### Before you open a pull request

- [ ] `pnpm lint` and `pnpm build` both pass
- [ ] The page still works with the network throttled to Slow 3G
- [ ] Saving a delivery twice does not create two records
- [ ] No `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` value was read outside `lib/`
- [ ] A failed request shows a readable message, not a blank screen
- [ ] Screens still match the design at 360 px wide
- [ ] Any change to a response shape was made in the shared types in `lib/`

## Design System

<div class="at-a-glance" markdown>

**In short:** this page defines the visual language of the Umucyo brand and its two products — the administrator dashboard and the agent app. It is the bridge between the design files in Figma and the code in the two frontends.

**Why it exists:** so that a colour, a corner radius or a heading weight is decided once, in one place, and every screen inherits it. Consistency is cheaper than taste.

**Source files:** [:material-figma: Group Sulwe — Figma workspace](https://www.figma.com/design/S72ejr0O70XSSEj0ZPGrPg/Group-Sulwe?node-id=0-1&p=f&t=a7AvV946QTD2nZmJ-0)

**Screenshots:** images on this page open full size when clicked. They use descriptive filenames in `docs/assets/design/` and `docs/assets/brand/`.

</div>

This section defines the visual language for the Umucyo brand and the products built on it. It serves as the bridge between the design files in Figma and the frontend implementation. By maintaining strict adherence to these guidelines, we ensure the product remains consistent, accessible, and aligned with the cooperative's day-to-day reality in the field.

### Figma source files

All raw design assets, wireframes and final UI screens are maintained in Figma. The links below provide access to the complete workspace used by the design team.

- [:material-figma: Umucyo sketches and wireframes](https://www.figma.com/design/S72ejr0O70XSSEj0ZPGrPg/Group-Sulwe?node-id=0-1&p=f&t=a7AvV946QTD2nZmJ-0)
- [:material-monitor-dashboard: Product UI — dashboard and agent app](https://www.figma.com/design/S72ejr0O70XSSEj0ZPGrPg/Group-Sulwe?node-id=0-1&p=f&t=a7AvV946QTD2nZmJ-0)

!!! note "Which one wins?"
    Figma is the source of truth for **appearance**. The code is the source of truth for **behaviour**. If a screenshot here and a running screen disagree, the running screen is right and this page is out of date — fix the page. If a colour here and Figma disagree, Figma is right.

### 1. Logos

The ecosystem uses two distinct marks. **Umucyo** represents the cooperative and the platform as a whole. The **Umucyo Agent** mark is used where the product needs to be identified separately — the installable app the collection agent keeps on their phone.

#### Umucyo (brand)

The primary brand logo features a geometric mark that echoes the shape of a weighing scale and the interlocking of grower and buyer, combined with the wordmark. It is set in the brand green and is the only mark used on official documents, signage and the documentation site.

#### Brand logo sketches and concepts

Early iterations and conceptual explorations for the Umucyo identity.

**Brand logo ideas**


#### Brand logo variations

The logo is adaptable across different backgrounds and contexts, ensuring brand recognition in all media. The variants below cover light, dark, single-colour and reversed usage.

**Brand logo variations**

[![Umucyo logo variations, palette, typography and controls](assets/design/style-guide.png)](assets/design/style-guide.png "Style guide")

#### Final brand logo

The finalised, approved mark for the cooperative.

The final approved mark is shown among the logo variations in the style-guide image above.

!!! warning "Do not recolour the mark"
    The logo may appear in brand green, white (on dark) or black. It may not be tinted with any other hue, given a gradient, rotated, outlined, or placed on a busy photograph without a solid panel behind it. If a background makes the mark hard to read, use the reversed version — do not add a drop shadow.

### 2. Style guide

The style guide covers the two products built on this brand: the **administrator dashboard** and the **agent app**. They share one design language and differ only in density — the dashboard is dense because it is used at a desk with a mouse; the agent app is airy because it is used outdoors, with one thumb, sometimes in gloves.

#### Typography

| Role | Face | Weight | Size | Line height | Where |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Display / page title | Roboto | 700 | 28–32 px | 1.25 | Page headings |
| Section heading | Roboto | 500 | 20 px | 1.3 | Card and form-section titles |
| Body | Roboto | 400 | 14–16 px | 1.6 | Paragraphs, table cells |
| Label | Roboto | 500 | 12–13 px | 1.4 | Form labels, table headers |
| Numeric / tabular | Roboto Mono | 400 | 14 px | 1.5 | Weights, amounts, phone numbers |
| Caption | Roboto | 400 | 12 px | 1.4 | Timestamps, helper text |

The rule behind the table: **anything a person might read back to someone else on the phone is set in the monospace face.** Weights, amounts and phone numbers are read aloud constantly at a collection point. Monospace keeps the digits aligned in columns and stops a `0` being mistaken for an `O`.


#### Colour palette

| Token | Hex | Used for |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `brand-700` | `#1B5E20` | Primary buttons, active nav, the logo |
| `brand-600` | `#2E7D32` | Hover state, links |
| `brand-50` | `#E8F5E9` | Selected rows, subtle fills, at-a-glance panels |
| `ink-900` | `#1A1A1A` | Headings and body text |
| `ink-600` | `#5F6368` | Secondary text, helper copy |
| `surface` | `#FFFFFF` | Cards, tables, sheets |
| `canvas` | `#F7F8F7` | Page background |
| `line` | `#E0E0E0` | Borders, dividers, table rules |
| `warning-600` | `#B26A00` | Pending SMS, preview states |
| `danger-600` | `#B3261E` | Failed payments, refused saves |
| `success-600` | `#1E7B34` | Paid, sent, completed |


!!! note "Check these against Figma"
    The values above match the Tailwind tokens used in the dashboard. If Figma has drifted, Figma wins — update this table and the config in the same commit, so the two never disagree by more than one pull request.

**Status colours are never used decoratively.** Green means *done*, amber means *waiting on something*, red means *needs a person*. If a screen shows red, somebody must be able to act on it. A red badge that means nothing is a support ticket waiting to happen.

#### Spacing, radius and elevation

| Token | Value | Used for |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `space-1` | 4 px | Icon-to-label gap |
| `space-2` | 8 px | Inside compact controls |
| `space-3` | 12 px | Between form fields |
| `space-4` | 16 px | Card padding on the agent app |
| `space-6` | 24 px | Card padding on the dashboard, page gutters |
| `radius-sm` | 6 px | Inputs, buttons, badges |
| `radius-md` | 10 px | Cards, dialogs |
| `shadow-1` | `0 1px 2px rgba(0,0,0,.06)` | Cards at rest |
| `shadow-2` | `0 4px 16px rgba(0,0,0,.10)` | Dialogs, dropdowns |

The scale is a 4 px grid. Nothing is positioned at 5, 7 or 13 px; if a design needs an odd number, the design is working around something that should be fixed in the layout instead.

#### Iconography

Icons are Material Symbols, outlined, 20 px on the dashboard and 24 px on the agent app. An icon never appears alone without a label on a destructive action — a bin with no word next to it will eventually be pressed by mistake.

